      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>2 MAKES THE McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR BEST PASTRY.</p>
        <p>out the important bearing which slum property and the poor sanitary arrangements in many of the schools must have on the health of the town. It was not simply house property which was responsible. His opinion was that the slums of Warrington were so bad that the children spent most of their time outside them. The removal of slum dwellings would tend to the health of the town and he asked the Health Committee to be persistent in their efforts to sweep away all insanitary property, whether it was owned by the Corporation or by private individuals. He was glad Alderman Bennett kept pegging away at the smoke nuisance. They did not want to injure the trade of the town, but to encourage employers and householders to minimise the nuisance, not simply from the health point of view, but from the sense of pleasant surroundings which all tended to healthy con- ditions of life. As to infantile mortality, he had been in a great many of the slums in St. John's Ward and had noticed how near the ash-bins were to the front doors of the houses and how improper the conditions of life were in many cases. He thought, however, that the chief reason for high infantile mortality in that ward was that there were no open spaces. Children were compelled to play in the streets and did not get fresh air. He felt that the Health Committee were trying to do their level best to deal with the problems. Councillor SKELLAND thought the greatest nuisances were drunkenness and laziness. There were some good houses in Warrington which had been made into slum- dons through lazy dirty people living in them. The Watch Committee ought to assist the Health Committee in getting rid of some of these evils, and try to teach people to be sober, honest, industrious and clean, as they did at the Ragged School. Councillor SINGLETON complimented Councillor Evans on his excellent report. With reference to his own trade, he (Councillor Singleton) as president of the Butchers' Association, was perfectly satisfied with the statement. Councillor Evans had said that if butchers would only comply with regulations there would be no necessity for a public abattoir. He (Coun- cillor Singleton) could assure the Council that he would do his level best to see that the regulations were carried out, and he would make it his special business to call a meeting of butchers and explain Councillor Evans' views to them. Councillor Singleton concluded by congratulating the Health officials on the diligent way in which they carried out their work. Councillor WILKINSON said Councillor Ashcroft had accused the Health Committee of having been asleep. If Councillor Ashcroft had not gone about the streets with a pair of motor spectacles on, he must have seen what the Health Committee had been doing. They had cleared away Pig Hill, at great expense, Silver square, one side of Silver-street,property in Dial-street, School Brow,Orchard-street, and Crossley-street. With regard to the smoke nuisance,they deplored the fact that smoke was at times belched forth from the chimneys when it might be regulated by the firing, but there was a vast differ- ence in the community when one could go to Bewsey Bridge and see the tall stacks standing smokeless. He could not stand the continual growling about the health of Warrington and the smoke question. If he was an outsider, he would not care about coming into the town when he read what was said about it. He bad Bewsey chimneys belching on to his house and other works were not far away, but last year he was able to grow some of the finest flowers that any man ever grew outside his house.</p>
        <p>Councillor HAWTHORN thought it was the Street Improvement Committee which had introduced some of the reforms to which Councillor Wilkinson had alluded. With regard to the smoke nuisance, there had been 9, disposition on the part of some to read into Alderman Bennett's words an import which they did not carry. His point all along was that the improvements he advocated would in the long run be of benefit financially to the manufacturers. It had been proved at Messrs. Crosfield's what a great saving could be effected by the adoption of up-to-date methods of dealing with the consumption of coal.</p>
        <p>Councillor EVANS: in reply, emphasised the effect which improper feeding of children must have on Infantile mortality. As to slum property he instanced an experiment by the Liverpool Corpora- tion who demolished some insanitary property and provided new dwellings for the people at Aintree. They found, however, that because the moral con- ditions of the people had not been changed they were fashioning slumdom in Aintree. It was more a moral question than any other kind of question. The Council had not entered upon a goodly heritage in regard to property and they were not responsible for all that had happened. He maintained that the Council wag alive to its duties and was quietly but persistently pursuing a course which was perfectly right and which would make for better conditions. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
