      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Warrington Receiver Smartly Punished.</head>
        <p>Warrington Receiver Smartly Punished.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Quarter Sessions on Tuesday, before Sir W. B. Forwood and other magistrates.—William Powers (31), furnacernen, furl Henry Perkins (36). labourer, previously of good character, pleaded guilty to breaking into the warehouse of the Whitecross Com- pany, Warrington, and stealing two new brass steps and other articles—in all 1191 b. in weight. Joseph Mattwews (53), metal dealer, of Warrington, pleaded not guilty to the charge of feloniously receiv- ing the brass, knowing the same to be stolen. Mr. Madden prosecuted, and Mr. Lindon Riley defended. _</p>
        <p>The brass was taken in from Powers and Perkins at the marine store of Matthews by his female assistant during his absence on Saturday evening, November 23rd. On his return from a public-house, at 11 p.m. Matthews found Powers and Perkins waiting to be paid. Acting on the statement that the weight of the brass was 811 b., he paid 3Gs., his calculation being is. against himself on the agreed price of ss. per lb. The brass was recovered after being consigned to Glasgow, Matthews swearing that he had never seen it, and was selling it with other brass at 51s. per cwt. The jury found Matthews guilty. Mr. Madden said the Whitecross Company had been obbed wholesale, and he thought Matthews ought to pay the costs of the prosecution as part of his penalty. The Chairman said he did not doubt that the ease with which Matthews would buy metal encouraged theft Matthews must go to prison for six months, and the other two prisoners each for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SHOP DOOR TEMPT.</head>
        <p>SHOP DOOR TEMPT.</p>
        <p>Elias Hurst (32), labourer, for stealing an overcoat from a shop door at Warrington on the 3rd inst., a man with a bad character,and who pleaded poverty,nine months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>"OUT OF WORK AND HUNGRY."</head>
        <p>"OUT OF WORK AND HUNGRY."</p>
        <p>Abel Smith (20) and George Ellis (20), labourers, breaking into a warehouse at Warrington and stealing brass, about £O, the property of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Limited, November 2nd. Prisoners' excuse was that they were out of work and hungry. Smith was sen- tenced for two months and Ellis (described as the ring- leader, and previously convicted) for four months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>HUMAN MACHINE,</head>
        <p>THE HUMAN MACHINE,</p>
        <p>HOW IT GETS OUT OF ORDER, AND HOW IT MAY BE PUT RIGHT AGAIN BY MOTHER SEIGEL'S SYRUP.</p>
        <p>In the finely-fitted mechanism of the human body each process is so dependent on the next, that when one jails your whole system is affected. And the most important process of all, the foundation of health, the very ground-work of life itself, is ,digestion. When this fails, nourishment fails, and naturally you become weak and ailing. Your liver breaks down in sympathy with your stomach, and the horrors of biliousness seize you. Then your bowels become constipated for want, of the natural laxative which the liver should supply and the outcome of It all is that impurities arising from these three causes—indigestion, biliousness and constipation—poison your blood and ruin your health. Mother Seigel's Syrup prevents or cures indigestion by strengthening your stomach, liver and bowels, and restoring them to perfect efficiency. Here is an example. Mrs. Jane Davis, 39, Herbert- street, Hurraby, Carlisle, writing first on April 17th, 1903, says : "I used to vomit till I was utterly worn out. I had pains in gt y stomach even after the vomiting, and fear- ful .headaches. I could eat nothing, and had no rest night or day, though worn out without fatigue. But Mother Seigel's Syrup cured me." Again, on May 6th of the current year, Mrs. Davis writes: "It is Sour years now since Mother Seigel's Syrup cured me, and lam still well and strong. I have never suffered since."</p>
        <p>Mother Seigsl's Syrup cures Indigestion, Biliousness Constipation, Headache. Wind, and all the other ills that arise from a disordered state of the digestive system.</p>
        <p>THE 2/6 BOTTLE CONTAINS THREE TIMES AS MUCH AS THE 01 SIZE.</p>
        <p>Mother Seigel's Syrup is now also prepared in Tablet form, and sold under the name of Mother Seigel's Syrup Tablets. PRICE 219—ONE SIZE ONLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LOCAL HECHABITISM.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HECHABITISM.</p>
        <p>Speech by Councillor Billam of</p>
        <p>Manchester.</p>
        <p>The members of the Charity Tent of the Warrington District of the Independent Order of Rechabites held their annual social gathering in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Saturday evening. The large attendance testified to the flourishing condition of the organization and the proceedings were highly successful. About 150 sat down to tea and afterwards a public meeting and concert took place. under the presidency of the Rev. F. Vaughan. The principal speaker was Councillor J. Billam, of Manchester, a prominent Rechabite worker. An admirable musical programme was submitted and all the items were highly appreciated. Songs were creditably interpreted by Mrs. Hannan, Miss Rachel Peacock, Mr. Hannan, and Mr. George Gough, and the voices of Mr. and Mrs. Hannan blended well in a duet. Concertina duets by Messrs. Stewart and Wild proved a popular feature and the humorous items by Mr. D. Sutton created general merriment. Mr. Fred Atkinson was an able accompanist. _</p>
        <p>Coll6Bior BILLAM delivered an impressive address in whica he said he had bee im Rechabite for nearly forty years, and that, he thofflght, was sufficiently long to test the value and utility of the organization. For many years he had taken an active part in Labour propaganda and had been returned to the City Council and appointed to the magisterial bench in the interests of Labour.fle did not know of any agency outside the work of the Christian Churnh which had been so successful from the standpoint of temperance propaganda as the Order of Rechabites. He did not know of any other agency which so really sustained earnest Christian temperance men, and he put it in that way because in the early days they used to be looked upon as men outside the pale of the Church—in fact as little better than infidels because they preached their doctrine in season and out of season. He thanked Gop that early in life he was brought to see that it was his duty to face the drink question, as the one question which impeded the extension of Christ's Kingdom upon earth. The Order, he believed, was an admirable lever to every Christian agency and one of the best aids to temperance reformation. He wondered sometimes whether the Church would ever realize to the full her indebtedness to the old temperance movement, to the men who had endeavoured to save the manhood of the nation from the terrible ravages of drink. The Recha- bites were entitled to the sympathy of all tempermice men and Christian workers. The Order was established in 1835. Whilst there were a few teetotallers at an earlier period, there was no such thing as organized teetotalism until 1831 or 1832. It was only the experi- ence of the Rechabites that chrystallised the beliefs in the advantages of total abstinence. The establishment , of the Rechabites led to the formation of the first ' temperance provident society in the country. Now every important life office recognized that it must have a separate section for abstainers. The Order was established with eleven members. To-day they had 250,000 adult and 200,000 juvenile members. (Applause.) Instead of teing in fear and trembling lest some one should come for money, they had , a million and threequarters to the good. (Applause.) The rules and ritual of the Rechabites were : printed in seven distinct languages. They had naval ; and military districts, and branches in India, South• Africa and Australia. They could rightly say that God had been with them and had used them as one of the instruments to perform His wonders. They were trans- ferring from their juvenile ranks 6,000 members annually to their adult tents, and yet increasing at the rate of 15,000 annually. Less than one per cent, of the men who joined the Rechabites ever violated the pledge. What a change there would be in the country if of all people who had signed the pledge one out of every , hundred had violated it! The nation would have been so different that we would hardly have been able to recognize it. To-day the Rechabites stood fourth of all the great affiliated orders of the United Kingdom. , They had registered more branches than. the four largest friendly societies combined for sixteen years in ' succession. (Applanse.) Out of every 100 branches. opened by the whole of the registered friendly societies •the Rechabites opened out fourth. A tent was never dis- solved without the members being guaranteed the funds they had invested. The last valuation showed that ; every "district was worth more then 20s. in the the ' average being 245. 11d. Councillor Billarn instanced the case of a man who drew sick pay from the Order for ' about fifty years. Continuing he said he did not think I Rechabites had fully realized their personal responsibil- ' ities and the splendid opportunity that now presented ; itself, when the workers were beginning to realize to a greater extent that drink was one of the greatest enemies they had to contend with. The Rechabites stood at the very highest point of solvency of all the friendly societies in the United Kingdom. Their tables were based upon the best actuarial authority. , They were practically the only society which could say not merely that it was solvent but talk about surpluses. One of the greatest reasons why Rechabites should throw themselves whole-heartedly into the movement was that through the juvenile organizations they were saving the manhood of the nation and on that account •they would be entitled to the gratitude not only of the temperance and Christian workers but of the nation. (Applause).</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN cordially congratulated the Rechabites on having such a magnificent scbeme and men like Councillor Billam who could set it before them so ably. He wished the movement every success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON PHILOMATHIC SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PHILOMATHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The History of the Labour Party.</p>
        <p>A well attended meeting of the above society was held on Wednesday, at which Mr. W. H. Stansfield delivered a most interesting and instruc- tive address upon the history of the Labour Party in England. The lecturer said that during the Saxon times every man was free and had a common share in the land, but after the Norman Conquest all land was claimed by the King. Part of this land was retained by the King while the remainder was let out to his Nobles who, in their turn, let a portion out. The tenants of the Nobles followed their land owners' example. This was a period of freedom. Limes, however, changed and the poorer classes became almost slaves. Guilds were formed and were of three kinds, merchants, crafts , and religious. From the first two kinds the present Trades Unions have sprung. After the formation of ! these guilds the standard of living of the working. classes gradually rose and was at its brightest in the year 1348, the year of the great plague. The great plague was a great blessing to the working classes,for it decreased the number of labourers and thereby raised ' the wages of the remainder. Numbers of people were enticed from the land to work in the woollen industry !and manufacture began to supersede agriculture. The growth of manufacturing industries led to the decay of certain towns and the growth of others : in fact the germs of our Modern Factory System had taken root. The industrial evolution changed the whole aspect of the labouring class. The advent of machinery and steam power caused sudden and violent changes, and these great inventions were all made in a comparatively short space of time. Our modern Factory System had fairly begun. England's wealth was increased ten-fold and gave her a start of 50 years in front of the Nations of Europe. These inventions were first applied to cotton manufacture, now our largest export. Formerly the workers had a direct interest in their work but now they became dependent upon the merchant or mill-owner. Women and children were able to do the work of roan; and they were in a worse condition socially and economically than ever before. Although during the Napoleonic wars the value of exports rose from £17,000,000 in 1793 to £58,000,000, the profits all went into the pockets of ' the capatalists, while taxation fell with especial severity upon the poor since taxes were placed upon every necessity and convenience of daily life. The degrada- tion and misery of the labouring classes further increased by the employment of pauper children which has been truly called "English Slavery." The condition of the labouring class had again reached a very low ebb, but the tide was again turning. The French Revolution and the Industrial Revolution had con- centrated men more and more closely together in the large centres of industry, and, although allowed to deliberate, they were not allowed to take ! any action. The results of these deliberations I aided in the passing of the Reform Bills of 1832, and 1 1867. The betterment of the working classes really I commenced with the advent of compulsory education 1 and Trades Unions during 1870-1884. The Trades Unions have done much to gain a greater measure of material prosperity for the working-classes. Power of Capital is now opposed by United Labour and some of the great strikes of recent years have shown how great this united strength of labour may be. A vote of thanks to Jr. Stansfield for his lecture, which was dis- cussed by the members, terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>.............-- ."...M1M....,..1,,,, . . . . . 4....... ..... ....... ... :,a'".l'..-=., . – ''' '.---.''2.-;ft'il,-.7.1;:p' r ' ..- '.- L'',-- . ' . .</head>
        <p>.............-- ."...M1M....,..1,,,, . . . . . 4....... ..... ....... ... :,a'".l'..-=., . - ''' '.---.''2.-;ft'il,-.7.1;:p' r ' ..- '.- L'',-- . ' . .</p>
        <p>The Examine'', Saturday December 14, 1907. WOMAN'S REALM. I I THE PUBLIC HEALTH.</p>
        <p>receive supplies from other districts where supervision may be imperfect or practically wanting. The need for thorough action in these respects being forced uniformly on all authorities throughout the country is being everywhere recognized, and the establishment of such a body as the recently proposed Supreme National Health Authority to take this subject among others in hand, would be of great service to the country. This scheme has been approved of by the Health Committee, which was represented at the Conference of the Sanitary Authorities of England and Wiles, recently convened to discuss the matter. Such an authority should be recognized by the Imperial Government, and one of the subjects it would have to deal with would be the com- pensation of cattle and dairy farmers or butchers for their voluntary surrender of diseased cattle or carcases. The expense of such surrenders at present falls on the farmers, and especially on the butchers, and it is only fair that the State should pay for its protection. With regard to the slaughter-houses in Warrington, the inspection is, I believe, adequate, but the sanitary con- dition of most of the premises is unsatisfactory. This has been in part due no doubt to the butchers being unwilling to make improvements in their own buildings so long as there was the possiblity of the establishment of a public slaughter-house and the consequent closure of their own. The only possible alternative to the establishment of a public abattoir is the recon- struction or such other improvement in the conditions of private slaughter-houses as will make them comply with modern sanitary requirements.' Councillor EvANs said that THE SMOKE QUES g had also been much discussed ' lately in Warrington and the Medical Officer wrote :—"The aealth Authority has been criticized for not dealing more actively with the nuisance. It must, however, be remembered that the authority's action is to a large extent limited by legal restrictions, and that the chimneys which are the worst offenders are for the most part outside its control. Since the Health Department was about two years ago made responsible for taking smoke observations, steps have from time to time been taken to minimize the nuisance, and in future all reasonable measures should be strictly enforced on manufacturers, &amp;c., to reduce to a possible minimum the amount of smoke given off from their works. As a matter of fact, I believe that most firms are at present attempting to deal with the matter from the side of economy, for black smoke means wasted fuel. But independently of that, I feel sure that the public spirited manufacturers of War- rington will, when the seriousness of the nuisince is definitely put before them, do all they can to abate it, especially when they see that the Health Authority, although determined to protect as far as possible the general public, is endeavouring at the same time not to unduly hamper the trades concerned." No doubt, said Councillor Evans in conclusion, the figures he had quoted might be challenged, but be did not think they could be proved to be incorrect. What he bad said set forth the general condition of the town as far as its health was concerned. He moved the confirma- tion of the minutes. Councillor BURTON seconded, and pointed out that there were some firms in Warrington who had spent thousands of pounds in an attempt to do away with the smoke nuisance—if it was a nuisance—but he wss doubtful whether it was. He did not know how they were going to abolish smoke altogether. It would be awkward if they did and it would be the ruin of War- rington. One thing, however, might be done and that was when manufacturers were building new chimneys they might build them a little higher. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>PARIS MILLINERY</p>
        <p>Parisian milliners have been busy, writes out Paris osrrespondent, preparing innumerable new models, with which the shop windows are already blooming. Azure tulle is a favourite material, as is also the diaddm of gold lace. Wreaths of small roses are on many a charming creation, and the uncurled ostrich plume, in possession of which the wearer can brave the terrors of dampness, is used often and with beautiful effect. Another ornament, which is more youthful looking and not less graceful, consists of a barette " of ribbon, holding on one side a bunch of roses and on the other side a bow. This graceful ornament makes the head- dress fuller; and is becoming to most faces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SMART COAT.</head>
        <p>A SMART COAT.</p>
        <p>Very practical and workmanlike is the smart long coat which is pictured in our sketch—just the thing for rough-weather wear, travelling, /hiving, or motoring, and a most useful addition to the winter wardrobe. As you will observe, this coat fits accurately in the back, but is cut with what one might call a close semi-fit, and half defines the figure in charming style. From the neok turn back wide revers and collar of the material, all finished with a line of machining at</p>
        <p>the edge. The revers are so arranged as to be capable of being turned up to the throat and fastened there in very bad weather. Though made with a wide wrap over in front, the coat fastens in single-breasted style, with two groups, three in each group, of large buttons. The sleeves are of the plainest coat type, and are finished with neat stitched and buttoned cuffs. Rough navy serge with a diagonal rib, heavy cloth, tweed, homespun, and frieze are all suit- able materials for the realisation of this design.</p>
