      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>IMPORTANT STATEMENT TO THE TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT STATEMENT TO THE TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Slum Property, Infantile Mortality, and the Smoke Nuisance.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening Councillor JAMES EVANS, in moving the adop- tion of the minutes of the Health Committee, gave his promised, statement on the health of the town. At a meeting of the Committee, the Medical Officer of Health submitted a report as to the necessity for the appointment of'a second female inspector and generally on the sanitary state of the borough,and it was resolved that the Chairman, the Deputy Chairman, Alderman Evans, and Councillor Dr, Joseph be appointed a sub- committee to consider the whole matter. Ten applica- tions for the tippointmenr of a female sanitary inspector were opened and referred to the Medical Officer of Health to tabulate. Councillor EVANS said the committee had gone to some trouble to obtain returns from 76 of the great towns of England and Wales. He would not read the whole of the report which had been prepared, but would hand it to the Press to make what extracts they liked from it :—The general death-rate per 1,000 popu- lation in 76 great towns was 15.9, Warrington 18.0 ; zymotic death-rate Ter 1,000 population, great towns 3.24, Warrington 310; infantile mortality rate per 1,000 births, great towns 145, Warrington 157. 'Front these figures Warrington appeared to have . A COMPARATIVELY HIGH DEATH-RATE .</p>
        <p>but it was unfair to compare the death-rates of a manufacturing town such as Warrington with the mean death-rates of the 76 great towns, many of which were not handicapped by those conditions which are inseparable from a manufacturing centre, and some of which were even natural health resorts, nor was it logical to specially ascribe the excess of the death-rates of a manufacturing town above these mean rates to "Very grave and sad defects in the management of the town." As a matter of fact the death-rates for Warring- ton ranged very near the average of those for other manufacturing towns, as is shown by the following figures :—General death-rate, 26 manufacturing towns 17-47. Warrington 180; zymotic death-rate, 26 towns 2'64, Warrington 310; infantile mortality rate, 26 towns 164, Warrington 157, _</p>
        <p>There was, however, no doubt as to there being ample margin for improvement in the general, zymotic, and infantile death-rates, both in Warrington and elsewhere. From the death returns for 1906 it was evident that two causes of death, tuberculosis, and diarrhcea, easily head the list,tuberculosis accounting for one eighth and diarrhcea for one-ninth of the total number of deaths in the borough during the year. As regards tuber- culosis, the Health Depaatment was at present attempt- ing to combat the disease in the following ways :—" (1) By obtaining voluntary notification of oases from medical practitioners and, by subsequent action, trying to limit the spread of the infection. (2) By disinfect- ing houses after the removal or death of a case. (3) By admitting a few patients into a hut at the , Aikin- street Hospital (it will only accommodate two at a time), and by showing these patients the ordinary conditions under which they should live and the measures they should adopt so as to avoid infecting others. (4) By preventing as far as possible the sale of tuberculous meat and milk." (Hear, hear, and applause.) More energetic measures in dealing with that disease must, however, be undertaken. A more thorough bacteriological examination of</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH'S MILK SUPPLIES had been recently instituted, but further control and supervision of all dairy farms throughout the country was needed, and should be enforced by Government uniformly on all authorities. The examination of the sputum and discharges from suspected cases of tuber- culosis would shortly be undertaken at the laboratory at present being fitted up at Aikin-street Hospital, thus aiding medical practitioners in the early detection of the disease. The opening of the Sankey Sanatorium for the treatment of early cases of the disease had been already discussed by the Health Committee, and that step should be taken as soon as possible, also some provision for isolating advanced cases (the extent at Present being twelve beds for paupers in the Workhouse Infirmary, and in that way limiting the spread of infec- tion from them to the healthy. The chief point as regarded diarrhgea was that it played a very important part in calming the high infantile death-rate. During 1906, out of a total of : 553 deaths of children under one year of age, 100 of these were certified as being due to that cause. The ' main factor bring-about these deaths was the WRONG FEEDING OF THESE INFANTS, either from ignorance or carelessness on the part of the mothers, and especially the substitution of the bottle. So long as the infant was suckled all went well, but when once that bad been given up the child had to run the gauntlet of not only improper food, but also of food which had become infected with those disease germs which lived and multiplied so readily amid insanitary surroundings. If the deaths of infants from that et-mac could be. obviated, then the infant mortality figures would be considerably reduced; and by adopting "The NotifiCatioe of Births Act," and appoint- ing an additional health visitor to visit the homes of these newly born infants and give instruction las to the feeding and care of the children Ito their mothers, a step towards reducing the infantile death-rate would have been taken by the borough. There were, of course, mothers, especially among the poorest classes, who had to go out to work and who were thus prevented from suckling their young infants, and that fact accounts in part for the higher infantile mortality in certain poor districts. In St. John's Ward the infantile rate was 155 for the whole ward, and 304 for the so-called slum areas, but the percentage of mothers going out to work was five times as great among the population of ,the slum areas as amongst the population of the whole ward. At the same time that they offered theoretical instruction they must also in many cases be able to offer practical help. He now came to the question of SLUM PROPERTY, about which much had leen recently said, and no doubt there was more of this class of property in the borough than was desirable as is the case in many other old towns. But, although the worst of this property had been and was being dealt with, it was impossible under present conditions to sweep it all away. There was a certain class of people who either from misfortune or more often from intemperance and other causes had sunk to the lowest social level, and who could not afford to pay a rent of four shillings a week ; and until that class was otherwise provided for—a problem that had hitherto baffled philanthropists and sanitarians in many other towns besides Warrington—it was impossible or at least inadvisable to clear away the more habitable of these cheaper dwellings. Such a course would only lead to several familes crowding together into the larger and perhaps more habitable houses, and to the origin of that most pernicious ,social condition of life in tenement dwellings, from which Warrington had hitherto kept free. Much, however, could be and was being done to improve temporarily a great part of the slum primerty. The enforcing of through ventilation in all houses, and of increased ventilation and light in many others, would make a great difference in their general sanitary condition. Still more was it necessary to try and influence the inhabitants themselves, who as a matter of fact, by their apparent love of dirt and by their dislike of even the limited amount of fresh air they were able to get caused no part of the unhealthiness of their dwellings. A local minister,,he read in the" Examiner," had asserted that the cause of the slurndom was either</p>
        <p>IGNORANCE, INTEMPERANCE, OR IDLENESS or, perhaps, all three, but he (Councillor Evans) con- tended that if the ministers would pay as many visits to the slum dwellings as the inspectors did there would be an altered condition of things. (Hear, hear.) He did not know that it was part of the work of the health authorities to displace ignorance, and to deal with drunkenness certainly he should object to have any- thing to do with idleness. He was not going to be responsible for all the lazy folks who did not work. He should leave that to the minister. (Laughter.) Then were in Warrington courts and narrow streets only 425 of those cheap slum houses, and the occupants numbered 1,316. That did not seem to be a great number in a population of over 70,000. (Hear, hear.) There were, as he had said, 425 of these houses standing. Councillor ASHCROFT : They want sweeping out. Councillor EVANS : I dare say my friend does not own any of them. (Laughter). Continuing, he said that on the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>QUE3TION OF THE ABATTOIR</head>
        <p>QUE3TION OF THE ABATTOIR</p>
        <p>a number of people seemed to think they would get to heaven when they bad got such a building. (Laughter.) Alderman PEERPOIST : No, no. Councillor EVANS sale this was what the Medical Officer of Health for the Borough had written about the suggested abattoir. " The question of the establishment of a public abattoir is under the consideration of the Health Committee, but even this step would not ensure the public that security from diseased meat which is expected from it by some. (Hear, hear.) The estab- lishment of a public abattoir and the closing of private slaughter-houses would undoubtedly facilitate the inspec- tion of meat killed within the borough but about one half the quantity consumed therein consists of animals which have been slaughtere,' and dressed outside the borough. The control of this imported meat, which is to a certain extent the greater source of danger to the public, would in no wise be helped by this apparently simple solution of the meat question. (Hear, hear.) In connection with both the milk and the meat supplies of any town or district, that town or district may thoroughly supervise its own cowsheds or dairies and also its slaughter-houses, but it it is still open to</p>
        <p>• 114 k , . AT; c</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Councillor AsHcuopr, referring to the question of slum property, said he believed the Health Committee had been asleep for years. He could take them into hundreds of houses that were not fits for pigs to live in. Let them look at some of the dwellings in Winwick- road, in Old Gardens, and parts of St. John's Ward. it was a shame and disgrace that people should have to live in such hluses. Alderman BENNE•rr said he was satisfied that the ' Health Committee were alive to the various ills from' which the comtuunity suffered, and that they were doing their best under the circumstances to find a remedy. The statistics presented were enough to cause any thoughtful man the greatest pain. One did not like I to see the continued recurrence of the high death-rate and when they thonght of the high infantile mortality, I it was a reproach not only to Warrington but to our common civilization. The evil was a great one, and it was not so much a matter of municipal administration ' as teaching the people the right way. Municipal administration and legislation between them could do : a great deal to accelerate progress and make the conditions under which many people were bound to live, an impossibility. He could not think that in any Christian community such slums as he could take them to ought to be allowed to exist. It was, of course, much easier to set forth fine ideals than to carry them into practice, but they wanted to actually realize in their hearts, minds and souls that these things were a scandal and an abomination, and then they would not rest until they had brought about the desired improvement. He agreed with Councillor Evans that model dwellings in the middle of the town were not a happy solution. He thought the remedy lay more in the direction of gradually getting people in the country and planting there in garden suburbs where they could live in comfort and in decent and civilized surroundings. He did not think the most degraded of the population lived in the slums for choice. It was very largely their misfortune. They had not been taught to appreciate anything better and there was a great deal of ignorance and drinking,but it, was not all. The question went much deeper and they bad a common responsibility as Christians and as citizens of a free country to their poorer brothers and sisters, and they would not have fulfilled the responsi- bility until they did their best to bring about a change, If the municipality had not sufficient power Parliament ought to step in. The building laws had been improved but they were not sufficiently drastic by any means. The time would come when it would be impossible for any builder to erect houses which were not sanitary or to put on a piece of land more houses than it ought properly to contain. The overcrowding which had seemed to be the necessary consequence of private enterprise up to now wanted checking and there ought to be Dower to ensure only a maximum number of houses on a given plot. As.to the milk and meat supplies, he was afraid he dare not trot out his solution or he would be called a free lover or an atheist or something of that kind. (Laughter.) Both of these questions had a serious effect on the health of the town, but he was sure the butchers and milk sellers were anxious to do all they oould to conduct their businesses under the most sanitary and satisfactory conditions,and that they would co-operate with the Health Committee. With regard to the smoke nuisance, he was surprised at the way, in which the medireval sort of notion that smoke was healthy and pleasant survived. A great deal of the smoke might be avoided. They did not wish to interfere with the welfare of the manufacturers, but he wanted them to realize there were methods now to enable them to dispense with a great deal of the dense black smoke which was emitted and at the same time effect satisfactory economies. He could not see how it was possible for the smoke to be good for the lungs, and it was certainly an absolute nuisance. If they brought the new Jerusalem itself and planted it on the banks of the Mersey and proceeded to turn out the smoke from Dallam forgeand other works, it would soon be more like the other place than the new Jerusalem. (Laughter.) No progressive citizen ought to rest content until something was done to minimise the nuisance to the greatest possible extent. ; Councillor PEMBMITON said that anyone who had a knowledge of manufacting towns would admit that War- rington stood very favoerably in regard to the smoke nuisance. If they went to Manchester, for instance, they would find new buildings blackened with smoke in twelve months. When doctors disagreed, what had lay- men to do? A very eminent medical authority in Warrington had said that smoke was not detrimental to health—that in fact it was rather good in some cases. Warrington was not a Cheltenham or a Scarborough but a manufacturing town. People did not come here to spend their holidays • it was a piece where money, was made to enable people to go holidaying elsewhere. As to the slum problem, did those who were always advocating nice houses, open spaces, Ac., consider what the workingman had to pay for them? .t was impos- sible for a man with 17s. per week, out of which he had to pay ss.for rent, to pay a larger rent in order to go to a better house. Jr he became ill and was ordered by the doctor to have chicken and port wine and a week a seaside, how could he afford it? The result was, he had to stay at home. What was required was a proper wage to enable a man to buy proper food. Model dwellings had been tried in some large towns and had proved a mistake, the death rate being as high as before. He agreed that slum houses ought to be swept away It was, however, a fact that people had lived ha houses which had been declared insanitary to be ninety years of age, and that emphasized his contention as to proper food. There should also be cheaper baths accommodation. He was dis- appointed with Councillor Evans' reference to the remarks of a minister as to overcrowding, ignorance and temperance being causes of the slum evil. He (Councillor Pembetton) understood it was one of the arguments put forward when the lady inspectors were appointed,that they were going to deal with the matter, by instructing the people in cleanly habits. He was thunder-struck when Councillor Evans said that duty should be undertaken by the clergy. The lady inspec- tors were the people to do it. :There was no doubt the Committee had got 26 of the nicest towns with which to compare Warrington' but even then our death rate was a serious question. Widnes had not a death rate likb Warrington. He too believed the Health Committee had been asleep, and he hoped they would push their duties forward.</p>
        <p>Councillor NIRLD thought the Council might safely leave the question of the abatement of smoke to the maaufacturers. The high price of coal had compelled manufacturers to see if they could not manage their business with a less consumption of fuei. Councilibr BALE, said that judging from the figures submitted., Warrington on the whole compared fay.our- ably with a large number of towns. The death-rate looked a high percentage, but when they compared the ratio of births and deaths.there was something in favour of ' Warrington. In the large towns the ratio was '57 but in Warrington it was only .51i. While they did not unfairly condemn their own town, they ought to realize that human life and its development,and progress were the finest wealth of any town and they should not be satisfied with the present death rate if they could reduce it. Dr. Gaffikin, in her recent report, pointed</p>
      </div>
