      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FESTERING &amp; POISONED WOUNDS. What a Simple Scratch May Lead to. Zam Buk, the Worlds Great Antiseptic.</head>
        <p>FESTERING &amp; POISONED WOUNDS. What a Simple Scratch May Lead to. Zam Buk, the Worlds Great Antiseptic.</p>
        <p>Nearly all festering sores or poisoned wounds can be traced back to some neglect. Any wound or break in the skin surface is liable to set up inflammation, painful swelling. iliscolouratiou, the secretion of matter, the formation of proud flesh, and the other familiar features of festering (suppuration) gathering, or poisoning.</p>
        <p>The obvious moral is : Don't neglect the tiniest abrasion or sore. Prompt dressing with Zam-Buk, which is perfectly antiseptic and a reliable germicide, will prevent complications. This pure herbal balm is eqnally effective when called into use to end trouble which has already been aggra- vated by neglect. Mrs. Flora Mac Kay, of Main Street, Greenock had blood-poising, ulcers, swelling and inflammation for 18 years through neglecting a seemingly simple scratch. Now, tAanks alone to Zarn-Buk, her skin is as healthy as that of a new-born child. The "Greenock Herald" made independent enquiries to confirm this extra- ordinary evidence of Zam-Buk's success, and Mrs. Mac Kay then said :</p>
        <p>"Eighteen years ago I scratched my leg with a thorn just a little above the ankle. It was merely a scratch, and I thought it would be alright in the morning, but the dye from my stocking trot into it and blooi poisoning ensued. My leg got swollen and discoloured and the tiny scratch became a very painful and inflamed wound, which discharged continually. "I suffered anguish with it, and often got no sleep. I was over three months in an infirmary, but the ordinary lotions and medicines used there were quite useless.</p>
        <p>"At last I read about Zam-Buk, and decided to try it. From the first the beneficial effects of Zam-Buk treatment were noticeable. The pain' and heat died away, and the wound got cleaner and healthier, and with continued applications of Zam-Buk I was com- pletely cured. Shortly after this I' had to be On busi- ness in the Highlands, and I walked nineteen miles in one day ! It is now two or three years since Zam-Bulc. Cured TMC, and my leg has given ne no trouble since." Do not neglect your skin. Commence the ideal Zam- Buk treatment to-day. Everyone praises its wonderful powers.</p>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION. The CHAIRMAN said he regarded Socialism much in the light of an ideal. It was an ideal in economics just as Christianity was an ideal in ethios. Christianity he did not think would ever be attained on this earth. He did not know whether Socialism would, but it was I Independent Labour Party. an ideal which would not be acoomplished in our days. Another of the series of meetings promoted by the It was too much of a revolution to be brought about in a reasonable time. The question arose when we arrived j Warrington Branch of the Independent Labour Party lat that ideal, should wehaveaocomplished the economic was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street on ideal that Mr. Williams spoke of? The best exposition of Sunday evening. There was a large attendance. Mrs. liberty was given by Herbert Spencer: "Each should Julia Dawson, a well-known Socialist writer, had been have freedom to do all that he will, provided that be announced to take the chair, but although she was infringes not the equal freedom of any other." That was ; the essence of the Sermon on the ount. 'Socialism resent she asked to be excused frofn officiating and M infringed that law of liberty. He tthe chairman) her place was taken by Mr. R. Pierce Williams. Mr. objected to be compelled to do anything or to be Ernest Clarke, of Stockport, gave an interesting restrained from doing something provided that his lantern lecture on "Slavery ancient and modern abstention or his action did not interfere with the and also exhibited a series of living pictures of scenes equal freedom of others: Socialism sought to in the Belfast strike. Portraits of prominent Socialists nationalise all the means of production, distribution and exchange. He himself was one of those means and he were thrown on to the screen.</p>
