      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 14,1907</p>
        <p>did not want to be nationalised. He did not want to be a mere cog in a huge industrial machine to be worked and oiled at the discretion of State officials. Under Socialism, in place of the present Government they would have a bureaucratic Government. Official- dom was to regulate the great State machine. The; dissemination of ideas would be curtailed if not altogether abolished. Then again, although we might , be a Communistic State, outside our own State we, would have to compete with individualistic nations. ; The question arose: Could this country support forty' or fifty millions of people without this foreign trade ? ! Mr. H. W. GURST said Liberalism still stood for The monthly meeting of the Town Council was held on Tuesday evenine in the Council Chamber under the peace, retrenchment and reform. The present Govern- presidency of the Mayor. There were also present : meet was carrying on reform in a quicker manner than had ever been known in the past, but no Government Aldermen A. Bennett, L. Ellison, John Evans, W. J. Forshaw, W. Pierpoint and D. Tinnion ; Councillors could go ahead of public opinion. In one way Social- W ists were helping the country to a solution of the T. H. Ashcroft, J. Ball, T. Barton, . Benson, T. Burton, J. T. Chantler, G. Craik, James Evans, J. Hawthorn, present economic difficulties but at the same time J. T. Locker, J. Lord, J. J. Nield, J. Pemberton, W. B. they were doing something to hinder it. They were Roberts, XV. Roeers rousing public opinion but they were misleading it. , Skelland, T. S. Sled J. Sankey, H. Singleton, J. , J. Fulton-Smith. 0. Toole, J. They were placing before the public an ideal : Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (town which was impracticable. Socialism violated the liberty clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin of the subject and another great objection was that it (borough surveyor), F. V. L. Mathias (electrical would give great opportunities to those in power to engineer), J. M.. Murray, (director of education), misuse that authority unless they were absolutely F. G. Ruddock (borough analyst), Dr. .1. C. Hibbert above-board. Whatever the system was, if the individuals (medical officer). were right the results would be satisfactory. But supposing they had a Socialistic State, they had got to , finality. There was no further progress to be made, and we would gradually go back to savagery. Socialists, he thought, were advocating a visionary policy which could not be attained. The more he considered the future held up to them by Socialism, the more it , appeared to hira that there would be great opportunities and temptations for unscrupulous people to pluck all the plums off the system and leave the dry oake for the scrupulous and confiding. He would ask Socialists to make sure that Socialism would not kill incentive to progressive work. He was unable to think that so long as the world existed there could be any perfect social system. Life must always be a struggle; some men must succeed on the failure of others. The State, however, must see to it that no one was born to failure, , that each had a fair start, with perfect liberty and equal opportunity to make the most of his natural abilities. The State must see that those who failed—because there must be failures— were decently provided for and that, according to the extent to which they had made use of their opportunities and abilities, they reoeived from the bare necessities of life to those oomforts which in some oases justice would seem to demand. That was what Liberalism stood for, and it would be a sad day for the country if thoughtless opposition or counter-attractions of ohimerical visions were to cause that ideal to be lost sight of. Mr. MORRALL said the ideal of Socialim might be right, but how were they going to attain it? The competitive system was acknowledged by most men at the head of affairs to create more work for the individual. Mr. R. Bell, in the recent railway dispute, said he did not require competition to be abolished, bemuse it would do away with hundreds and hundreds of men's employment. If they re-organised industry and abolished competition what would they do with the men who were thrown out of work? Were they going to shorten the hours of labour to such an extent that they would find them all employment ? He believed Socialism to be an impossibility. Under Liberalism they had some immediate reform, and why not take as much of that as they could get? Mr. BURTT said modern evolutionary science had shown that all progress depended upon struggle or con- flict of some kind, and in human society, if there wits to be any progress, there must be competition. If that were done away with,the individuals of the nation would deteriorate in capacity and the result would be that the nation itself as an economic unit would deteriorate in efficiency. Liberals realised the evils that existed quite as strongly as Socialists, but what was the fundamental cause? Mr. Williams overlooked the most important factor, which was the land. It had been shown by Henry George and others that private ownership of land was at the bottom of our economic evils. He (14,1r.Burtt) main- tained! that if that were removed the present economic evils would disappear. He was glad Mr. Williams did not insult them by showing them that Socialismhad nothing to do with atheism and free love. Mr. ROBERT JONES said he did not see how they could eliminate competition because even in a Socialistic state there would be high positions to be bestowed, where one man would have the power over others, and there would naturally be competition for , them. He contended that Liberalism would place com- petition on a fair basis by giving equality of oppor- tunity. Mr. KENDRICK said it would appear that while the process of nationalisation was going on the man who was out of work would be no better off. They would be striving at something which was really not a present need. They wanted to do something to immediately relieve those who needed help most. Mr. J. MORTON said that if they examined the political or constructive aspect of Socialism, as distinct from the ideal, and compared it with Liberalism, they would find there was very little difference. Mr. Williams said the main distinction between Liberalism and Socialism, was that Liberalism was striving to keep the competitive system intact while Socialism would abolish it. He (Mr. Morton) did not think that was a fair description. It was Liberalism that first put the system of municipalisation into operation. For his own part, he did not regard municipalsstion as a tendency to Socialism. It would lead eventually to sometning very different—to a state of affairs in which hureaubracy would have much greater power. He did not agree that competition must exist if progress were to continue, and therefore it became a question of organizing means by which the competitive system need not exist. If the Socialists could tell them how to accomplish that end, he was with them. Until then, each man must adhere to his own convictions and work accordingly. Whatever scheme was adopted for bring- ing the competitive system on to a co-operative basis, that could be taken up by the Liberal party just as easily and probably as well as by any other party. The central idea of Socialism, he took it, was the organizing of industries whereby each individual shonld receive the full reward of his labour. If that was the central idea, he did not care what surrounded it,whetber it was State ownership of the means of production, or whether it was a system of private industry. Mr. MOVES contested the idea advanced by Mr. Guest that Socialism having been attained, the nation would retrogress. He believed that under Socialism they would be "happy ever afterwards." The debate was adjourned until next Monday evening.</p>
        <p>COLT'NCIL'S LOSSES. The MAYOR said : It is my painful duty to announce the death of two ex-Mayors of the borough. I doubt whether any occupant of the chair has ever had Ito perform such a painful duty. I have to announce, I almost in one breath, the deaths of two of the oldest , members of the Council. One of them was, in fact. I although he had just left us, the " Father of the Council," and it seems almost correct to describe him still as" Mr. Alderman Francomb." I should find it difficult to divide my thoughts or my words in speaking of two such useful, worthy, and honourable men. Both of them were not only aldermen together, but they were magistrates, and each of them had filled the office of Mayor. I doubt, in their particular spheres, whether the Council ever possessed more useful and energetic members. (Hesr, hear.) It may be said of them that they were whole-hearted in their work, doing their utmost for the welfare of the town, and each of them has left tangible evidence and lasting memorials of the good and useful work they die It only remains for me to move the Council to express a sense of its loss and its condolence with those whom these gentlemen have left to mourn their loss. I have to move: "That this Council receives with deep regret the announcement of the death of James Fairclough, Esq., an honorary freeman, alderman and justice of the peace, and formerly Mayor of the borough, and desires to record the great loss which the borough has sustained by his death, and to express its warm appreciation of his valuable continuous public services extending over a period of 16 years. And that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to the widow and family of the late Mr. Fairclough the deep and sincere sympathy of the Council with them in their great bereavement.' Alderman PIERPOINT seconded. He said it was his lot to do so as the present " Father of the Council." He did not know whether he was entitled to that honour, but some of his friends told him that he was. At all events, he had been a member of the Council sufficiently long to know how both Alderman Fairclough and Mr. Francomb did their work. Alderman Fair- dough was a thoroughly honest English gentleman, and did his work efficiently as an alderman of the borough. Of Alderman Francomb he would only say that in his day and generation he had done good work. Of both he would say," Well done, good and faithful servants." (Hear, hear.) The resolution was carried in silence, all standing. In moving the minutes of the Finance Committee for the first time as its actual chairman, Alderman BENNETT paid a high tribute to the late Alderman Fairclongh. His blunt,straightforward honesty, his shrewd, practical common-sense his business qualities and experience,and the zeal which he threw into his duties as chairman of the committee and as an overseer of the poor, as well as in numerous other public capacities, had been of infinite service to the town ; and they had all lost a friend and comrade whom they would not readily be able to replace. (Hear, hear.) In moving the adoption of the minutes of the Gas Committee, Alderman PIERPOINT said he did so with ' profound regret, for their old and respected colleague (Alderman Fairclough) had passed away "to that bourne from whence no traveller returns." He was sure that all of them mourned the loss of one who gave many years of good work in the service of the borough, and for which he earned the thanks and admiration of all. (Hear, hear.) He occupied the chair of that Com- mittee for many years, and carried out the duties with a patient diligence, marked ability, and such gentle consideration for the members, as to secure their loving respect in its fullest sense. The MaYoR moved : " That this Council receives with deep regret the announcement of the death of Richard Walker Francomb, Esq., a justice of the peace, and formerly Mayor and an alderman of the borough, and desires to record the great loss which the borough has sustained by his death and to express its warm appreciation of his valuable continuous public services extending over a period of 27 years. And that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to the family of the late Mr. Francomb the deep and sincere sympathy of the Council with them in their great bereavement.;' Alderman BENNETT seconded, and said it was with a peculiar sense of obligation that he did so, as it was to Mr. Francomb be owed his entrance into the Council. This resolution was carried in a similar manner to the previous one. THE BEAMONT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the Education Committee came</p>
        <p>up for confirmation some discussion occurred, at the instance of Councillor SKELLAND, on the resolution that £76 10s. be paid to Mr. C. W. Davenport, the con- tractor for the erection of the Beamont School, in settlement of his claim for extra pumping in connection with the excavations of the foundations.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor FULTON-SMITH said the matter was one of those unforeseen and unfortunate circumstances which happened in nearly every undertaking. There had been an influx of water which could not be provided against and after the building was finiahed the influx was still going on. Consequently extra work had 'to be done. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that the charge was made in respect of excavations for the foundations and not for the pumping after the school had been opened. He felt strongly that the Committee should have been informed of the matter earlier and given an opportunity of agreeing upon a price. Councillor WILKINSON said the contractor's claim was for £l5O, but they had been able to settle it for £76. The minutes were approved. THE LATE MR. H. P. BRIERLEY. ALL CLAIMS TO BE PAID.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee a letter was read from Messrs. H. B. White and Sons, stating that they had been requested by the widow and relatives of the late Mr. H. P. Brierley to inform the committee that it was their wish that no part of any loss which might have arisen from the late Mr. H. P. Brierley's acts or defaults should fall upon the rate- payers of the borough of Warrington, and they authorized them to state that they were prepared to pay in full all claims which could be shown to them to be justly due. Alderman BENNETT, in moving the minutes of the Finance Committee, referred to the letter with regard to the late Mr. H. P. Brierley. The responsible departments, he said, were still engaged in investigating the extent of the deficiencies, which were very considerable, and they were not yet in a position to make a detailed statement, but they would all agree with him that the action of Mr. Brierley's wife and family, and of the firm of solicitors who repre- sented them, reflected the greatest credit on them all, and was in accordance with the highest standard of private honour and of public obligations, and had done something to relieve the pain of the incident which all of them would willingly forget. (Hear, hear.) He might add that every care was being taken to avoid the possibility of any similar occurrence in the future. Councillor \VILKINSON seeonded. Councillor CRANTLER asked how it had come about that an official of the Corporation, who, according to the evidence given at the inquest, was not supposed to receive money, had been able to handle it. He thought it was a very serious matter. The MAYOR : I think it would be better to let this matter pass for the present. It will come up for dis- cussion again. Alderman BENNETT : The committee are going most carefully into the whole of the circumstances in con- junction with another committee. I can assure you that every possible pain is being taken to get at the root of the whole thing and to avoid anything of the kind occurring again. The minutes were then agreed to. At a later stage of the meeting, when the minutes of the Health Committee were under consideration, Councillor CHANTLER alluded to the following resolution :—" That a horse, trap, and harness be pur- chased from Mr. Brierley's executors for the use of the I department at the price of £60." He wished to know if the horse, trap and harness had been purchased after advertisement according to the standing orders. Councillor EVANS replied that they had not, but the ' circumstances were too painful to allude to further. t The horse, &amp;c., had, however, been secured in the best ; interests of the ratepayers. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN "WASTREL." At Runcorn on Monday William Pollitt, of kltrin- cham, whom the magistrates described as a wastrel," was sentenced to two months' hard labour for neglect- ing his four year old crippled son. :qrs. Whitby, a relative, had had care of the boy since his birth, and the prisoner had not paid a penny this year towards his maintenance, and was altogether in arrears about £l5. It was stated that the prisoner spent his money in drink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SOMALISM &amp; LIBERALISM.</head>
        <p>SOMALISM &amp; LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING DISCUSSION BY JUNIOR LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association, held at Gregory's Cafe on Monday evening, when a discussion on' Socialism and Liberalism" was introduced by Mr. R. Pierce Williams. Conncillor J. Hawthorn presided. Mr. 'PIERCE WILLIAMS said that in view of the probability that the question of Socialism and Liberalism would engage the minds of progressive men in a grow- ing degree, and of the possibility that political events would bring them to a point where they must either support Socialism, or if they were to remain active politicians, become associated with a party whose basis of common action would be opposition to it, he welcomed the opportunity of speaking to them on that subject. It was to be deplored that a cool considera- tion of Socialism was made the less likely owing to the declamations of its opponents. Macaulay spoke some- where of "details which make up a picture in which nothing is wanting but facts," and if he (Mr. Williams) might adapt that, he would say that some men gave details which macie,up a picture of Socialism in which nothing was wanting but Socialism. Let them see what THE ESSENCE AND AIM OF SOCIALISM were. What one economist described as representingin the shortest possible formula the aim of Socialism was given by him as follows :—" To replace the system of private capital by a system of collectivA capital—that is by a method of production which would introduce a unified organisation of collective or common ownership of the means of production by all the members of Society." It is not my purpose, continued Mr. Williams, hcre to examine what the effect of such a revolutionary social reorganisation would be, only to say that the present system of competition would be removed, private trade and enterprise for the purpose of profit would be at an end, industry would be nationally equipped, and socially managed. Turning to Liberalism. I would say, without implying anything objectionable, that it is difficult to define what present day Liberalism is. Liberalism has been described as an attitude of mind. This is true in the abstract. But it is true of other isms, and for our purpose we require a less inconcrete, a more tangible definition, one which would be peculiar to the set of principles which are called Liberalism, and one also that is consistent with its declared policy. We have a number of declarations which will assist us in our consideration of</p>
        <p>LIBERA,LISM. Sir William Harcourt, although he once said that" we were all Socialist now," yet declared that the Liberals had no revolutionary scheme in politics, in society or in trade. Toynbee, the Radical economist,a considerable number of years ago said that "Liberals had advanced to this position: that where the individual rights conflict with the interests of the community,there the State ought to interfere ; and second, that where the people are unable to provide a thing for themselves, and that thing is of primary social importance, then the State should interfere and provide it for them." How far this is a departure from the old Liberal creed, Toynbee well knew, for he also says that the old Radicals disliked State interfeTence, and had complete faith in the people's ability to help themselves. But events have been admittedly far too strong for the strict Individualism of the old Liberal Party, and on August 26th, (1907) the" Daily News" said that the Liberal Party had shed the last rags of its creed of laissez-faire. Yet, if you take Mr. Haldane as an exponent of Liberalism, the Liberal Party is not yet willing to adopt those measures which Socialism declares to be essential. For, speaking in October (1906), he said "If in the name of Labour, Keir Hardie, or anybody else, brought forward abstract propositions which con- flicted with Liberal principles, if he wished to nationalise land or capital or anything else the Liberal Party would cross swords with him." Here we have a definite statement that the nationalisation of land, or capital or anything else conflicts with the principles of Liberalism. But a greater than Mr. Haldane, Mr. Gladstone said that he freely owned that compulsory expropriationis admissibleand even sound in principle. ILiberalism, I assume, is</p>
        <p>AN ATTEMPT TO REMOVE SOCIAL EVILS without causing an organic or fundamental change in the structure of Society. Resuming our consideration of Socialism, WA find there is no ambiguity about its policy and aim. Distinctly and unequivocally it declares that it seeks to remove the present system of private and competinr capitals, and substitute one of collective and co-operative capital. Its main economic idea is the transformation of private capital into collective capital. Short of this orga ic change Socialism alarms that any number of reforms will prove but palliatives. In order to effect the change it will be necessary to</p>
        <p>TRANSFER PROPERTY NOW HELD BY INDIVIDUALS, to the State, or organised Society, and it will be need- ful for the State to undertake the organisation of labour and the work of distribution. From these certain mis- conceptions have arisen, and on them are based some of the misrepresentations of Socialism with which I will now deal. The means to be adopted to transfer private capital to the ownership oi the community have been incorrectly donoribad as Confiscation; and here is one of those charges against Socialism that has no basis in fact Expropriation of individual capital would be carried out by compensating its present owners. Another wrong deduction that has been made is that Socialism intends to abolish private property. another false representation of Socialism is that it is oppsed to Capital. According to the Hon. Alfred Lyttleton who said that" he would endeavour to examine Socialism with candour," "the basal founda- tion of it was hatred and horror of Capital." Mr. Williams dealt at some length with the nature and formation of capital which, he contended, was stored Labour. Socialism, he continued, does not condemn Capital, but only its appropriation through the opera- tion of the anti-social principle of the private owner- ship of the instruments of production. Socialism does not ignore Capital as an economic factor. It recog- nises that it is indispensable. But It claims that its efficiency would be increased if it were col:ective, and, manifestly, by making it co-operative, the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ENORMOUS WORK</head>
        <p>ENORMOUS WORK</p>
        <p>which competition and misdirection of private capitals entail, would he stopped. Turning to other charges made against Socialism, such as interference with the family and home life, and a rigid system of regulation that would result in an intolerable limitation of indi- vidual freedom, these would not necessarily result from Socialism. Tney are what may be termed accre- tions and accessories of it. If oppression there will be, then, as It. Lowell says," every man will be his own own oppressor." Socialism contends that this freedom does not exist to the large mass of men at present, and avowedly aims at removing artificial restraints on a man's opportunities of development,to provide equality 'of opportunity, varying only with his natural abilities. Aa to the old error with regard to Socialism of dividing up, this is so obviously contra- dictory to the positive principle of Socialism that it bears its own refutation. The touchstone of Liberalism and Socialism is their ability to bring social conditions into harmony with accepted rules of political economy, and if this cannot be done except by a fundamental re-organisation, then the comparative value of each as a policy, is determined by its intention to deal with the system itself. If Liberalism will not remove a system under which consumption is not the chief aim of industry ; if it declines to touch in its essentials. a system that ignore' that the fundamental aim should be the satisfaction of wants at a minimum cost of life ; if it will not take steps to secure man's material independ- ence; if it upholds a system where conflict of interests is predominant; then it fails as a social policy. But, if Socialism, by organization, will approach economy and harmony of production ; if its policy is to make consumption the end of human exertion ; if, by making the many responsible to the one for the pro- vision of employment it will make him materially independent; if it seeks to remove the competitive 'element from industry, then Socialism must be admitted to produce an economic remedy that Liberalism fails to supply. We have seen that the main positive idea of Socialism is the socialisation of the instruments of production, including, of course, an adequate and efficient provision of such instruments; and also the organisation of labour. And these it claims would abolish unemployment .and poverty. It therefore provides a remedy where Liberalism fails. Taking a deeper conception of the social problem, supplying an economic theory of Society that fits in with facts, it would a/Sply a scientific principle. Stand- ing, as someone has said, as a conscious effort to organise a</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE COMMONWEALTH, it is, as Gonnor says, more than the approval of graduated taxation and as a system of factory legisla- tion; it is a definite scheme of economic organisation. Not predicting its exact methods in the fixture, yet it. is but the extension and general application of a principle already in operation in our municipalities and qublic departments. Described by Vandeivelde as the ideal extension as well as the organic completion of present tendencies, it is the recognition of the trend of social development, it is an intelligent anticipation of the operation of social forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTES TO THE LATE ALDERMAN FAIRGLOUGH AND MR. R. W. FAANGOMB.</p>
        <p>A PRESCRTPTTON FOR KIDNEY TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>A severe pain in the lower region of the back on rising in the morning is a sure sign of kidney trouble. Don't neglect this sign, but try a course of the follow- ing medicine, which is recommended by a high medical authority :—Tincture Buchu, one ounce; Karox Com- pound, one ounce; Syrup of Lemon, two ounces. Take a teaspoonful in a wineglass of water three times daily after meals and at bedtime. Any good chemist will dispense this for you. Take care to use ingredients L exactly as prescribed.</p>
