      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 14, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>./ :t</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>DIE:104.8BR 70, 1907.</p>
        <p>The Small Holdings Act is THE LABOURER a great and surprising AND THE LAND. success. We have accounts</p>
        <p>from different sources of the eagerness of the English farm labourer to get a piece of land to till. In some counties the applicants for land are begging that no more meetings explanatory of the new legislation may be held, seeing that applications have been made for all the laud that is available; and in one case,</p>
        <p>Earl CARRINGTON tells us a whole county has been applied for. This means, of course, that there will be many cases of disappointment. So much land hunger exists that there do not exit the means of fully satisfying it. What a commentary this is on the protestations of the landowner.. in the House of Lords! The Small Holdings Bill, it was said, was an I, unnecessary measure; it was not framed to meet any genuine demand; the farm-labourer slid not</p>
        <p>_ want to work land on his own account; if he d his landlord was quite ready to meet him, and would let him have as much land as he liked within reasonable measure. And so on and so on. It was once remarked of a Peer who was a Gold- Stick-in-Waiting that " the silly old man did not even know his own silly old business." Obviously the Peers were either ignorant of the feeling of the peasantry in regard to the land or they were pretending. Landowning is their busioess.DJ they understand so ill as to have honestly fallen into this gigantic error about there being no actual demand among the rural labourers for cultivable land? We find it uncommonly hard to believe so. Another allegation was that the rural labourer could not farm land properly if he got it. There may be some truth in that—even a good deal of truth ; but experience so far is highly encourag- ing. In respect both to land of his own and to Crown land, Lord CARRINGTON has carried out extensive experiments in peasant cultivation. He, told the Central Chamber of Agriculture the other night that he has put over 3,000 small tenants on his own estate, and in forty years, on 23,000 acres of land, he has only lost nineteen farm tenants Of their own free will. This indicates an exception- ally small proportion of failure. On the Crown lands there are between 3,000 and 4,000 acres farmed by small tenants, and there is every reasan to think that they will be farmed successfully in almost all cases. The advantage to the land- owner will be as great as to the tenant. A small holding implies a higher rent, a consideration which would probably have led the Peers heartily to support the bill had it not been for another consideration which was rather plainly hinted at by Lord LANSDOWNE. The owners of the land did not want to lose their bold of the labourer, They wanted to do" what they liked with their own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>wT,?,</head>
        <p>wT,?,</p>
        <p>-.‘ 7</p>
        <p>_ - .</p>
        <p>.77.7</p>
        <p>The Small Holdings Act has introduced into the agricultural districts not only a most encouraging outlook, but such prospective freedom as has not been known there since the days of our Saxon</p>
        <p>forefathers. The new conditions introduced into the labourer's life will not, of course, put an end to the exodus to the towns. Nothing can do that. The population will continue to be too thick for the soil. Among the peasant proprietors of France only one son is brought up to farm work. The others must go into the army or into trade. And it will be the same among ourselves in the case of the small holders. They will be unable to provide for all their children on their holdings. There will be a periodical breaking away from the family. It happens everywhere in agricultural districts, and is, indeed, inevitable. But though the exodus cannot be stayed, its volume must be considerably diminished. The old village life will come back again, with some of the old village trades. The greatest trouble that will arise in connection with the new conditions will, doubtless, have reference to cottages. No landlord wants to build houses for agricultural labourers. They do not pay. The humblest cottage cannot be built for much less than £250 if it is to be fit to live in, and the interest on that amount is much too heavy a rent for the rural labourer to pay. Here, again, Lord CARRINGTON has been a pioneer. He lets cottages at a shilling a week, losing on them in one way, of course, but finding himself recouped by the higher rent he obtains from the farmers on the ground that there is always labour at their doors. Possibly in connection with the Small Holdings Act some co-operative scheme may develop which will enable the small holder to become the owner of his own house.</p>
        <p>Mr. McKenna annoys the A WORTHLESS "Church Times" by his CONCESSION. "endless iteration " of</p>
        <p>popular control and free- dom from tests. Our contemporary says: "We have long since conceded these. By all means let there be full popular control, and let appointments be made without tests." Such a statement is not worth much, for if the "Church Times " has made such a concession, the Church which it represents has not. But the supposed concession is really no concession at all, for our contemporary goes on to say: " What we demand is that our children shall be taught the religion of their parents in school hours by teachers of whose fitness to give such teaching some proof shall be required." This means that in single school districts, where the only school is a "Church school," tests for teachers will still be maintained—and there are thousands of such schools.</p>
        <p>We hope there will be a THE INFIRMARY ready and complete response ErrENSION. to an appeal which is being issued this week-end for subscriptions to complete the balance of E 1,2,50 still required for the Warrington Infirmary Extension. The new buildings are rapidly approaching completion, and already the Nurses' Home has been brought into use, while it is hoped that the formal opening of the new Wards will take place in the early Spring. The amount for which the committee appealed in the first instance has nearly been reached, a result which is viewed with keen satisfaction. As the result of the extension, however, it has been thought desirable to make certain altera- tions in the old buildings to meet the new conditions, which have involved the expenditure of about £l,OOO in excess of the original estimate, The Committee therefore earnestly appeal to all those who have not already subscribed to assist in the work, and so make it possible to open the buildings free of debt.</p>
        <p>Not very long ago this MR. VICTOR gentleman visited Warring- GRAYSON, M.P. ton as a Socialist demagogue and spoke at a small meeting. Warrington was not interested. But since that day Victor Grayson has become the Victor of Colne Valley election and the idol of the Socialist Party. He came to the front at a bound and then seriously disturbed the decorum of the British people by the famous broken bottle speech in reference to the Belfast riots. Victor was. wrong in letting himself go on that occasion when the passion of the populace was already roused. He entered Parliament and spurned it with his victorious heel almost simultaneously. Now he is stumping the country and disrespectfully tearing to pieces nunierous time-honoured institutions in the name of Socialism. Victor Grayson is essentially a young man in a hurry—so were Lloyd George and Winston Churchill—and he is none the less interesting on that account. He delivered one of the wittiest and mcst bitingly sarcastic speeches in Parr Hall on Wednesday that we have heard for a long time, and he kept his audi- ence on the tip-toe of expectat;on and interest throughout. Yet the speech was a disappoint- ment to many, and particularly to those who went to learn. While not without evidences of deep earnestness and occasional flights of real eloquence, the speech was lacking on the constructive side and, like Mr. C. E. Parker who contributes an interesting letter in another column, we were sorry that Mr. Grayson devoted so much of his time to the discussion of the pamphlets which were distributed outside the hall. It would have been much more satisfactory had be applied himself seriously to elaborating the social reforms and ideals dearest to the hearts of the Socialists. The error into which Socialists most commonly fall is that they show us how to pull down when what we want is an inkling of the way they would build up in the far-future Socialist state.</p>
        <p>Councillor Evans' promised THE HEALTH OF statement on the health of THE TOWN. the town and the causes of</p>
        <p>the high death rate has at last been presented to the ToWn Council, and we must congratulate the Chairman of the Health Committee on the trouble he has obviously taken to make his report both understandable and instructive. The state of the public health is a wide question, and the causes of the more apparent deficiencies call for no small amount of investiga- tion and thought. Councillor Evans made it gait() clear that his Committee was fully alive now to the necessity for an active campaign against various long-standing evils which have had much to do with the high death rate of the borough both infantile and general. Judging by what the Committee is prepared to do as the result of the application of the spur, we should conclude that there is some truth in the charge that for some time they have been, if not asleep, at any rate lethargic and inactive. Councillor Evans made it quite clear that there is a very wide field for strenuous labour on the part of his committee, and the pro- gramme outlined indicates that they will have to keep their noses to the grindstone for a consider- able time in ordlr to effect any material improve- ment in the condition of things. Comparisons show that•the death rate under both the general and infantile mortality heads is higher in War- rington than the average for the 76 great towns of England and Wales. To get rid of this unenvi- able distinction will be no light task in itse'f, for it involves, as Councillor Evans showed, close supervision of tuberculous disease, a thorough overbight of the milk and meat supplies of the town, the combating of improper rearing by poor and ignorant parents, and the rigorous demolition of slum property. There is no doubt that the Health Committee will need con- siderable voluntary help—if only to teach the misses that life itself is largely dependent on temperance and cleanliness. It was wisely pointed out that the mere removal of slum areas will not permanently improve the lot of the very poor; their habits must undergo a change to correspond with the improved surroundings or a recurrence of the trouble will be only a matter of time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>. . • - - ' .</p>
        <p>F.c„ ;A!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The police promotions which we announced last week have given general satisfaction. We all like to see merit rewarded and there is a consensus of opinion that the officers who succeed to higher rank are capable and hard-working and thoroughly deserve the advance. The discernment which the authorities have shown in making the promotion is commendable. * * One way to Heaven is through the public abattoir, according to Councillor James Evans. Perhaps the souls of the slaughtered beasts have a quick transit to the Promised Land, but this is not what the speaker meant. He was criticising the suggested abattoir for Warrington and said " A number of persons think we shall get to Heaven when we have a public abattoir !" * Sheer capacity and industry have won for Councillors Pemberton and Lord the positions of Deputy Chairmen of the Paving and Sewerage Committee and the Markets Committee respectively. It is not long since they were isolated from the bulk of the members owing to their strong attitude on the Non-Trading principle, but time has proved that they are both men of ability and thoroughness in municipal work and no two members are now listened to with closer attention.</p>
        <p>e, The Warrington Junior Liberals run their Associa- tion on right lines, for their is no attempt to narrow the views advanced at there meetings. On Monday evening Mr. R. Pierce Williams, an avowed Socialist, gave an interesting paper on "Socialism and Liberalism "which gave rise to an equally interesting discussion. It is worthy of note that in the discussions the Liberals, unlike most Tory critics, refrained from imputing unworthy motives to the Socialists and criticised the proposals purely from the economic and constructive standpoints. * * * Speaking at the discussion on the public health at the Council meeting on Tuesday, Council Ashcroft said : " There are hundreds of houses in Warrington which are unfit for pigs to live in." Nobody questioned the statement, so we suppose it is an accepted if deplorable fact. Rather curiously, the chairman of the Socialist meeting in Parr Hall the same evening also made a pointed allusion to pigs and property. He said—and the remark was applauded with vigour— that "there was a place not far from Warrington where pigs were living under conditions which would be a God-send to thousands of human beings." This 'is true enough, and 'tis a pity that pigs and people I cannot change places. But the poor we always have with us, whereas prize pigs are not bred in a day ! A woman in time is one in nine.—MIZNER.</p>
        <p>*---* *</p>
        <p>The simple and childish virtues of veracity and honesty, the root of all that is sublime in character.— ntuasoN.</p>
        <p>One obtains success to-day more by the noise that one makes than by the talent that one has.—EDOCARD PAILEROIL</p>
        <p>Political reputations are made by saying what you link, and they are kept by saying what you don't think !—MRs. CRkIGIE.</p>
        <p>Equality creates an atmosphere which kills imita- tion, and is pregnant with originality ; for every one acts out himself, having nothing to gain by imitating anyone eIse.—BELLAMEY. I never bet on the man who is always telling what he would have did if he had bin tha,re ; I have noticed that this kind never git OMIT . —JOSH BILLINGS. When women cease to meddle, the world will become a stagnant pool, tideless and windless, with the ship of humanity rotting idly on its shore.— CHARLES LEE.</p>
        <p>Should the Germans conquer England—it is always the Germans that we have in our eye, though it is not considered prudent to say so—l daresay the victors and the vanquished would settle down after a bit. When the editors of the Radical newspapers had all been put in gaol and he who shouts, "Late again, Teddy! " whenever the King fails in punctuality had been hanged at Newgate ; when English beef came within the schedule of poisons and German sausage was the "only wear" ; when our spouses were called " Frau " instead of "Mrs.," and the boys in the streets cried " Iloch ! " instead f " Wot cheer ! " no doubt we should have a cricket match of old and new Englishmen at the Oval, and a football tussle at the Palace for a German silver mug. No conqueror can make a nation wholly unhappy. So the people feeling nervous because the Rosyth Naval base—where the quarter sessions is Rosyth?—will take ten years to construct, may lay that flattering unction to their souls and get a little sleep o' nights.—" London Opinion."</p>
        <p>G * Last Sunday and Monday were "red letter days " of the history. of Guildism at Padgate Wesleyan Church, for the services n were nt only excellent and inspiring, but they were attended in large numbers by members of the various Guilds connected with the local Wesleyan Churches. As the membership of these Guilds numbers close upon 1,000, and is com- posed (so some consider) of the "flower of maidenhood and manhood " it is easy to foretell what a great force they will have in course of time, upon the religious, moral, and social life of the district. •* * * Very little comment has so far been made either in the Town Council or the Press with regard to the circumstances leading up to the lamentable suicide of Mr. H. P. Brierley, a clerk in the Warrington Borough Surveyor's Department. It was tacitly admitted that nothing but harm could come of a prema- ture discussion of this painful matter, but it is certainly getting time the public was afforded some explanation of the condition of affairs which led to Ur. Brierley's tragic atonement. We all know that there were serious cash deficiencies—which the relatives of the deceased have offered to make good from a sense of "private honour and public obligation," as Alderman Bennett so well put it at Tuesday's Council meeting. He promised an early statement as the result of investi- gations which are being made. There must have been a serious lack of proper supervision in some quarter, for the poor fellow now deceased to have got into the difficulties which he did with regard to public moneys. • No 'doubt "steps will be taken to prevent</p>
        <p>any such occurrence in 'the future," but whaf every- body wants to know is : Why was the stable-door left open so long?</p>
      </div>
