      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in 'rucking it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>NEEDS AND EARNINGS UNDER SOCIALISM. A CHALLENGE TO THE WARRINGTON. SOCIALISTS.</p>
        <p>Question. "What principles are to rule the reward a man is to get for his labour in the Socialistic State John Workman desires to establish and how are these principles to be harmonized in their application to the question of division? What plan does he propose to adopt to make those do their fair share who show neither faith- fulness nor enegy ?', Answer. "The first essential is that each shall give accord- ins to his ability. If each individual was compelled to work accord- ing to his ability, efficiency and industry would be common. And if all men gave of their best, why should they not receive according to their needs ?" After waiting five weeks we have at last got the above answer to my thrice repeated question. It is somewhat- strange that with John Workman's ability for stringing columns of words together and indulging in vague generalizations he seems incap- able of giving a direct answer that is free from ambiguity and that a plain man can understand. It appears that this is the only answer we shall get so must make the best of it. After stripping what is superfluous as above, I gather from his answer that . a man's needs are to be the only measure of a man's share of the common stock, that efficiency and industry are to have no part in determining this share, but that these qualities will somehow or other be operative, and that if they are not they are to be brought into existence by com- pulsion. Let us take the parts of the reply and see what we can make of them. Take the 'first section of the answer, "The first essential is that each shall give according to his ability." This raises the whole difficulty because this condition which John Workman says is essental and takes for granted, will not exist under his Socialistic scheme. In the second sentence John Workman shows that he has no faith in his own assumption. In the first he assumed that men give according to their ability. In the second they are to be compelled to work. This reminds me of the proclamation of a South American President who stated that " Every able bodied man was to volunteer under pain of death." The difficulty here is as great as that raised in the first sentence because he assumes that it would be possible for Society to compel men to work accord- ing to their ability which we cannot accept without proof. In the third sentence we have a further assumption that men will "give of their best." We have here three assumptions none of which John Workman is able to show would exist in his Socialistic state. In _ other words John Workman assumes the necessary conditions and having, without a word of proof, taken these for granted, harmonizes the difficulty of paying a man according to his needs on the assumption that efficiency and industry would be forthcoming, or if not forthcoming could be attained by compulsion. We have a right to ask what motive is to take the place of the present proportionate reward which man gets for his labour, and if that motive, whatever it is, fails, what method of compul- sion does John Workman propose to use to compel efficiency and industry? It is not a sufficient answer to quote a Biblical expression as all Socialists do, that if a man does not work neither shall he eat. The question is not between work and idleness, but between efficiency and industry as against inefficiency, slackness and indifference. Take the question of efficiency in the light of John Workman's answer, "If each individual was com- pelled to work according to his ability, efficiency and industry would be common." It is a great deal more likely that efficiency would be very uncommon. The efficient and industrious would suffer under a sense of injustice and the inefficient and phlegmatic would enjoy the opportunity of benefiting by the pooling system. The motive at present in operation which works for efficiency and industry is the increase of the r.3ward. John Workman's plan admits of no increase except by means of an enlarged stomach. There is to be no association between efficiency and reward, only between needs and receipts. I am not in any way surprised at John Workman's lame effort to meet the difficulty. It is the same problem which every Socialist.has to face, it goes to the root of the ques- tion and the Socialist attitude towards it is always the same. It is like that of a Scotch Divine who in approaching a difficult passage addressed his hearers thus :—" My dear friends, this is a difficult text, very difficult, but friends let us not be discouraged or cast down,let us look the difficulty fairly in the face and pass on." The only point of weakness in the illustration is in this, that very few Socialists will even go the length of looking this difficulty fairly in the face. Like John. Workman they turn away their faces, hark back and find fault with the present system which we all can do with great ease. I have surely said enough in this correspondence to show that I consider our present conditions far from perfect, but the imperfections are in the applica- tion of the principles not in the principles themselves, The principle that every man gets in proportion to the worth of his labour is the governing one in our present system. In so far as the application of this principle is imperfect, the system requires adjustment, and I am willing to co-operate with any man, whatever he may call himself, who honestly and wisely seeks to right what is wrong. But Socialists of John Workman's type ask us to adopt a principle that has no sound ethical or economic basis, and when challenged do not seem to have the remotest idea how the neces- sary conditions to make the principle work can be attained. Such men are an impediment in the progress of the nation and race ; they block the wq of those who have a clear idea of not only what is wrong but of how it can be put right. Our advice to such is to stand aside that others may go forward. To stand out of the way until they know not only what is wanted, but also how it is to be attained. Sir T. P. Whittaker puts this aspect well when be says" My trouble is that the propaganda diverts atten- tion from the real sources of trouble and difficulty, causes them to neglect genuine and practicable remedies, tempts the people to drop the substance for the shadow, and makes advance along the path of true progress and reform far slower and more difficult than it need be were it not that earnest and loveable souls, who should be helping us, are frittering away their time and strength in pursuing a will-o'-the-wisp which is leading them into a bog of impracticable nonsense." This correspondence has dragged on already too long; we have had enough words. Let us put the question to the test of act and see whether John Workman is prepared to put his creed into his deed. John Work- man and his supporters believe thata man should be paid according to his needs. Then it they have any faith in their creed I challenge them to put it to the test. Let John Workman and nineteen of his associates make a common pool of their earnings. Let a committee of three or four be appointed by the twenty to manage this common fund and divide it according to the needs of the members. Pali of the Committee's work would be to alter the proportions from time to time. If to the house of No. 12 a baby comes, No. 12 must have more and the others less &amp;c. If No. 15 through his industry and efficiency receives a rise in wage or salary the increase must go into the common fund and only one twentieth be his and so on. It is easy to see that this experiment will have a much better chance of success than if the scheme were national and included a strong minority opposed to it. John Workman will have the opportunity of selecting nineteen convinced enthusiasts like himself who believe in the soundness of the principle. To give a little interest to the experiment I am willing to give, at the end of every completed year in which the Association holds together, £lOO, say :t5O to the Warrington Nursing Association, and 1,50 to the Infirmary. The only condition I lay down is that the experi- ment must be a genuine one, the whole earnings of each unit must go into the pool and the need of each must be the only factor determining the distribution. To be assured of this I would ask my friend Alderman Bennett to make a yearly investigatiou of the payments in and out. -.4?..!.A PIACI 1404 CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, December 10th, 1907. LABOR OMNIA. VINTIT.—We have been compelled to hold your contribution over until next week owing to the pressure of Christmas advertising.—Ed. "Ex;</p>
        <p>- ::e</p>
      </div>
