      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>We hear with pleasure that the following gentlemen have offered to give any advice or assistance in their power to members of the Society on the culture of plants. They will welcome any inquiries either verbal or written. 'Mr. J. Longland, Egypt-street ; Mr. J. Fairhurst, Grappenhall ; Mr. W. H. Woodcock, Stockton Heath; Mr. 3. Ryan, Stockton Heath; Mr. F. H. Longshaw. 192, Manchester-road ; Mr. Whitfield (head gardener),Bank Park; Mr. G. H. Smith, 13,Loyely-lane. We are sure that this intimation will be welcomed by the members of the Society and that many will avail themselves of the help RO kindly proffered. The tree planting day at the Bearnont school was a very impressive function though rather spoiled towards the end by heavy rain. The school gardens are laid out on an extensive snale and the whole business is being taken up by teachers and children in a spirit of enthusiasm which augurs well for the future. The surroundings of the school will be a pleasant feature in a neighbourhood which will soon be completely built over. The sympathy of all on- members will be extended to our friends at Stockton Heath in the loss they have sustained through damage being inflicted upon a large number of trees. It is to be hoped that the perpetrators of this act of wilful and wanton destruction will be discovered and just punishment meted out to them. The annual tree planting day of our Society will be held, as will be seen from the advertisement column, on Friday next, the 20th inst. The proceedings will com- mence at 2.30 p.m. in Egypt-street, where several trees will be pianted. Then it is anticipated that a few will be planted near the new Infirmary buildings. It was originally intended to plant a line of trees in front of the institution hut this has been found to be impractic- able owing to the numerous pipes, itc., below the sur- face of the footpath. From the Infirmary a move will be made to Manchester-road where further planting will be carried out. The Infirmary and Manchester-road planting has been very kindly arranged and the trees provided by the Warrington Corporation. These efforts are highly appreciated by the Committee of the Society. Members and friends interested in the movement are cordially invited to be present next Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>CURED AND STILL CURED.</head>
        <p>CURED AND STILL CURED.</p>
        <p>CHAS. FORD 6'S Bile Beans do not</p>
        <p>Merely Relieve—They Cure Permanently. Confirmed Lancashire Evidence.</p>
        <p>Chas. Forde's bile beans have earned their world-wide reputa- tion as the finest of all family medicines, solely by their proved value, and the happiness they have given to millions of people—the life-long in valids they have brought •to health, the workers, disabled by pain, they have restored to their wage-earning position in life, the homes the. have changed from abodes of sickness to bright, cheery family circles. The secret is that Chas. Forde's bile beans cure people thoroughly with cures that are lasting.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. Ware, of 99," Conway-street, Birken- head, says :—" For a long time I suffered everely from indigestion and piles. The worst pain was in the middle of the back—a pain that seemed to numb the whole of my body. I also had mite rheumatism in the shoulders, and could not raise my arm. I could not eat or sleep, and I lost flesh rapidly. _ _</p>
        <p>Wlien T commenced to take Chas. Forde's bile beans my condition was very serious, but I had not long been taking the medicine before I felt a decided improvement. The pains diminished, my spirits revived, and my appetite gradually returned. I continued to take Chas. Forde's bile beans until my stomach had quite recovered from the disorders and all my ailments were cured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>STILL WELL TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>STILL WELL TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>It is more than two years since Mrs. Ware gave her testimony. A few days ago she wrote :—" Since Chas. Forde's bile beans cured me I have enjoyed good health, which I think is splendid considering I am in my sixtieth year."</p>
        <p>Chase after a Cyclist. At Crewe Police Court on Tueeday James Brorn, ,of Warrington, was charged with stealing a bicycl the property of Joseph Charlesworth. The machine was left outside the Horse Shoe Hotel, and he saw the accused riding away along with another man. He gave chase, and succeeded in overtaking the accused, who said that people ought not to leave bicycles out- side public-houses and tempt people to steal. He was remanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE CHOIR Concert InTh-e—Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE CHOIR Concert InTh-e—Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The seventh annual concert of the Warrington Male I Voice Choir, given in the Parr Hall on Monday, attracted, as it deserved to do, quite a large gathering. In addition to the choir's own work, and it was by no means inconsiderable, the attractions were offered of Miss Lucy Davies, Madame Annie Grew and Mr. Fowler Barton as vocalists, young Mr. Bradford Barton as violinist and Mr. Thornlev-Dodge as society enter- tainer. Collectively the efforts of this concert-party resulted in a very interesting evening being spent, although much of the music, and this applies especially to the songs, was of too doleful a kind to afford exactly vvh.:t, one expects to find provided on these occasions, that is, entertainment. The ensemble singing of the fifty odd male vocalists who practice under the joint-conductorship of Mr. Nesbitt and Mr. Hassall is already widely known for its fine qualities of tone, precision and expression, and all that the choir did en Monday showed that a high standard is not only now always maintained, but that the essentials to success just mentioned have now become integral factors, as it were, of the choir's con- stitution. For although there were occasionally some unsteadiness and haziness in the lighter pieces—" The Franklyn's Dogge " and "John Barleycorn "—this was probably due rather to a carelessness born of familiarity than to insufficiency of rehearsals, because in the bigger and more onerous things like "The Phantom Host" and "The destruction of Gaza" there was little to take exception to, the attack being often magnificent and the taking up of disjointed parts always easy and free from jerkiness. Of the presentment of Hegar's dramatic choral ballad it would be difficult to speak too highly. From its massive unisons and ponderous harmonies, its wailing and dirge-like phrases, and its dignified contrapuntal passages there was built up a rendering so grimly and wierdly realistic as to be ' positively fascinating, and there can be little doubt that in this piece the choir found something worthy its highest efforts, and worked and perfected it accordingly. Madame Annie Grew's rendering of sevsral songs such as "Dear Heart !" (Matta) and those bracketed as amn your dear eyes "and "The rose will blow" VMS distinctly disappointing inasmuch as the exaggerated emotionalism which characterised it defeated its purpose of arousing the listener's sympathy, becoming on the contrary a source of some irritation. This is the more to be regretted as Madame Grew is endowed with a contralto voice of large range and good quality and in the use she makes of it flexibility and good taste are otherwise elgarly apparent. Miss Lucy Davies set herself a difficult and somewhat thankless task in pre- senting " Johanna's song" from Tschaikowsky's "Maid of Orleans," but the ease end clearness with which she sang it and the dignified restraint by which the effort was marked not only showed that she was equil to its demands but merited all praise. The songs by Mr. Fowler Burton were both well sung and well chosen. "Glorious Devon" and "The little Galway Cloak" being especially worth of note as fine specimens of artistic vocalisatioa which the audience thoroughly enjoyed. Mr. Bar on has distinctly improved as a violin executant in the interval which has elapsed since we last heard him, so much so indeed that while his capabilities as a soloist are yet in a plastic state one feels not a little concerned as to whether present promise may be fulfilled in latermaturity. Mr.Thornley-Dodge's contributions directed towards enlivening an evening in which, is hen been said, the prevailing note was one of unnecessary dejection, were as highly successful as they were undoubtedly superior to anything we have heard in this line since the time now long past when the late Mr. Mel. B. Spnrr was a familiar and welcome tivirri at the Parr Hall Saturday night concerts. Mr. Dodge's lightning charcoal sketches and his original penes such as" The Matinee Hat "and" Baby Bill "may be cited as indicating a deftness in caricature drawing, an artistic imagination and a literary facility rarely thus happily combined in one and the same individual. Mr. W. H. Payton. Mira. Bac., acted as accompanist in that skilful and unobtrusive way which always supports yet never overwhelms the most exacting and fastidious of vocalists and instru:nentalists.</p>
        <p>A CHRISTMAS REMINDER TO TALKING MACHINISTS.</p>
        <p>Already, in an earlier issue, special mention has been made of the delightful Christmas records issued by the IColumbia Phonograph Company, of 64 and 66, Oxford- street, London, W. Now that the festive season is close upon us a reminder of these good things will be welcomed by any talking machinist who has not yet written for the artistic Christmas catalogue which the Columbia Company offer to send free to all ingairiers. This interesting little publication should certainly be in the bands of all. It cantains a capital collection of seasonable selections on both disc and cylinder records applicable to all makes and styles of talking machines ; and the arrangement of the nnmi,ers in the form of a series of proiatthrnes will be found most helpful by the many taking machinists who are just now planning social evenings. Then, the Columbia Company are further catering for the needs of the season by bringing out their list of January titles before Chestmas. This list, which is now re vb., is full of the most attractive and up-to-date titles of every kind Am 10-inch, 12-inch, and the popular 10-inch double-face discs at 3s. each. The record user .its thus afforded a wide choice of the' latest successes for the holiday season, and be has only to get the Colombia "January list " and " Christmas Programmes" front the address given above to ensure the best of good times to himself and friends.</p>
        <p>RUG3Y FOOTBALL. - By " Ovalist." , Warrington advanced another step up the League ladder on Saturday over the mangled corpse of the Swinton lion. Time was when there was no team so , feared as the Swinton team, hut they have fallen upon lean times of late, and afford easy victims to most clubs to-day. The locals were "spoiling for a fight" as the saying goes, after a fortnight's inactivity, and they set about Swinton to such effect that 26 points were run up by brilliant football before the interval. In the second half there was some relaxation of effort, and only another seven points ammed. The final figures were 6 goals, 7 tries (33 points) to nil —the biggest score Warrington have ever put upon against the Blues. Last year they ran up 29 points to nothing, so they have gone one better this time. Saturday was the first occasion this season on which Warrington have topped the 30 points!</p>
        <p>Anybody seeing Warrington up to hall-time on Saturday would have imagined that they had tumbled across the finest Rugby team in the universe. The play all round was brilliant in the extreme, and tries followed one another in such rapid order that the efforts of the defenders seemed to be of no account at all. Much of the scoring was due almost entirely to the work of Jenkins, who played in absolutely his best form and gave the threequarters such opportuni- ties that they couid hardly help shining in combina- tion. The passing was very fast and accurate and the whole team seemed in such form that it was a pity the opposition was not of a sterner character. Oldham, Hunslet or Wigan would have had to take a back seat at Wilderspool on Saturday, I think.</p>
        <p>Fish re-appeared on the left wing and scored a couple of tries in smart style, but he was so clearly out of condition that he could hardly be considered an acquisition. The probability is that if Kenyon had been fit he would have got the preference. Master Jack willhave to do some more training if he wishes to appear against the All Blacks next week. On the other wing Brooks played with rare dash and was a a very difficult man to sty. All the threequarters crossed the Swinton line on Saturday —a flue tribute to their attacking powers.</p>
        <p>Swinton cut such a poor figure that one is liable to misjudge them. I can harlly imagine they are as bad as they seemed on Saturday, for there were no evidences of class whatever. For one thing, the men lack physique, while several are in the rawest state of inexperience. The halves seemed particularly green, and were " diddled " with surprising ease by Lees and Jenkins. They will have to learn to tackle before they do anything else. Townson played a fair game at full back, and A. Valentine was occasionally useful, but he does not possess a shadoNs of the family greatness.</p>
        <p>The Northern League table up-to-date is as follows: A gst. Per-</p>
        <p>For.</p>
        <p>" P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c 9 tl•ge. Hunslet Oldham Wigan ll 9 1 127 38 168 12 11 57 86.36 Keighley l3 10 3 032 29 151 20 15 85 7612 Hull l2 8 4 0 30 30 150 19 14 80 66.66 Warrington Halifax Broughton Batley l4 9 5 032 33 163 21 25 117 61'85 Barrow Wakefield T ll 6 4 127 30 144 19 20 98 59.09 Salford Hull K. R. .13 7 6 034 36 178 18 27 117 53.84 Bradford l3 7 6 0 20 28 124 16 28 116 53'84 Leigh Dewsbury l2 5 7 0 15 18 84 21 27 123 41'66 Leeds Runcorn ll 4 7 0 14 19 85 14 19 85 3635 Bmmley l2 4 8 0 16 16 80 34 41 191 33.3$ York Huddersfield Rochdale ll 3 8 Gll 17 73 25 24 122 2727 St. Helens l3 3 9 1 14 19 Frs 23 54 208 26.02 Widnes l2 2 8 2 10 23 89 22 32 140 2500 Swinton Ebbw Vale l2 2 9 1 10 11 53 23 44 178 2083. Merthyr</p>
        <p>The Lancashire League table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For Agit. P. W. L. D. P. G. T. Pt&amp; G. T. Pts. Warrington Oldham 7 6 0 1 13 15 27 111 4 5 23 Wigan Salford 8 5 2 1 11 16 2i 95 10 10 50 Barrow 9 5 4 010 918 72 818 70 Leigh Broughton Runcorn Widnes Swinton St. Helens Rochdale</p>
        <p>Warrington play the return match at Oldham to-day and mean to make a great effort to reverse the verdict of October sth, when Oldham won at Wilderspool by 110 points to 7 point. On that occasion the home I team was minus Fish, Brooks, and Taylor, and for nearly half the game Tilley was off through injury. Seeing that Oldham have not yet been defeated, there seems small chance of Warrington's success, hut I am convinced that the team selected to do duty is equal to any side the Oldham club can put in the field— and it proved superior in the Cup Final. A good deal depends on the luck of the game, and it is certainly Warrington's turn, for the Oldham men had all the lack at Wilderspool. Anyhow, Warrington will not be defeated for want of trying their hardest.</p>
        <p>The team is : Tilley, full hack ; Fish, Dickinson, Taylor and Brooks, threequirters ; Lees and Jenkins, half backs ; Thomas, Boardman, Shugars, Belton, Harmer and Naylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>The men are reporfed to he in the pink ofeondition, and evpn Fish is getting into fighting trim by the aid of turkish baths and training. There should be a battle royul at Watersheddlngs to-day.</p>
        <p>The " A " teams of the two clubs meet to-day at Wildetspool, and the contest should be hardly less interesting than the one between the seniors. War- rinaton "A " are the only team which has won at Watersheddings this season, and they should be equal to getting another two points from to-day's encounter. It is pleasing to note that Isherwood is well' again, and will assist the second string. The team is : Jordan, fall hack; Landor, Isherwood. Cartwright, and Tinkler, three-quarters ; Lloyd and Daintith, half baelc.; ; Boardman, Kettle, Jolley, Forster, Preston and Nforley, forwards. A new man is being tried in the person of Kettle, a well-built scrummager from Salford St. Bart's.</p>
        <p>THE ALL BLACKS VISIT.</p>
        <p>FULL DETAILS OF THE ARRA.NGENIENTS.</p>
        <p>The preparations are being made for the entertain- ment of the New Zealanders on Saturday next, and given fine weather.a 500 gate should be assured, the ground has been banked up at the railway end, and the stands and rails have all been strengthened.</p>
        <p>The All Blacks arrive at the Central Station from Manchester at 10.50 on Saturday morning, and will probably be received by the Mayor at the Town Hall. A four horse bus will meet them at the station with the Warrington officials and Committee. After the reception, the team will go to their head quarters at the Lion Motel for lunch at about 12 o'clock. At two o'clock they will leave for the ground. On entering the playing arena, they will, be greeted with welcoming cheers from the Warrington players, and will respond w:th the customary Maori war cry. Then to business for an exciting hour and a half. The All Blacks are keen on beating the Cup holders, and will meet a team as keen as themselves.</p>
        <p>After the match there will be high tea at the Lion )tel, the whole contingent, together with Warrington players, officials, committee, and guests being enter- tained by the Warrington Club at 6 o'clock. At 9 o'clock the party will adjourn to the Hippodrome for the second show.</p>
        <p>The New Zealanders, to the number of 29, will remain the guests of the Warrington Club until Monday morning. Weather permitting, they will go for a walk in the morning and a drive to Knutsford in the afternoon with tea at the Royal George Hotel. On hionday morning it is hoped they will have time to !my a visit to Messrs. Crosfield's works, before leaving "for I\fanchester at 11.30.</p>
        <p>To-day they meet Halifax at Hanson-lane, on Wednesday Yorkshire at Wakefield, on Saturday Warrington, and on the following Thursday (Boxing Day) Hunslet at Parkside, concluding a heavy pro- gramme for the year with a visit to Salford.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday' December 14, 1907. MS IN GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>noises</head>
        <p>noises</p>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>CHESTER HARRIERS v. WARRINGTON ATHLETIC CLUB.</p>
        <p>Warrington visited Chester on Saturday last, for their first inter club fixture with the newly-formed Chester Club, who enjoy the patronage of His Grace the Duke of Westminster. A strong pack started from the Ermine Hotel and followed a somewhat tricky trail, over about six and a half miles in the neighbourhood of Hoole, the greater portion being on the roads. How- ever about two miles of heavy country, interspersed with obstacles of the stiff order, provided variety suffi- cient to satisfy the most fastidious, and some of the runners gained experience which should prove of great service on future occasions. A. G. Roberts (Chester) was first man home in 39min. 59sec5. ; J. Bolt (Chester) 2nd, 40min. 2secs. ; H. Holbrook 3rd, 40min. 9secs. ; G. Watmough, 4th; M. Tavlin, sth; S. K. Hayward (Chester), 6th; A. Ford, 7th; J. T. Mullhall, Bth ; S. D. Parsonage, 9th; C. Brown (Chester), 10th; R. C. Woods (Chester), 11th; J. Brown (Chester), 12th. In a count of five aside the points were distributed thus :—Warrington: 3,4, 5,7, B—total 27. Chester: 1,2, 6, 10, 11—total 30.—a win for Warrington by the small margin of 3 points. Despite the unwelcome attentions of a nail, which had to be extracted from one of his pumps after finishing, Holbrook was rapidly overhauling the leaders at the finish. Hayward, the enthusiastic secretary of the Chester Club, came with a rare burst in the last half mite, and succeeded in splitting the Warrington pack. With experienced helpers like A. G. Roberts (late of Sefton), and J. Bolt (late of Preston, and formerly of Runcorn), "the purest form of sport" should be developed in the Chester district. Warrington have accepted the invitation of Broughton Harriers to join in the combined run with Sefton and Sutton Harriers, from Heaton Park, the country quarters of the Broughton club, on Saturday next, 21st inst. Local Derby to-day : Sankey Harriers from Bridge Foot. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Her Trembling Body. St. Vitus' Dance complete!y cured in</head>
        <p>Her Trembling Body. St. Vitus' Dance complete!y cured in</p>
        <p>the case of a Bilston girl by Dr. WillFans' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>A representative of the "Birmingham Echo" recently paid a visit to 24, Temple-street, Bilston, and interviewed Mr. and Mrs. Fellows, who keep the White Rose Inn, and who have been well-known and highly-respected residents of the district for close upon thirty years. He then gathered details of the remarkable cure of their daughter, Susannah, of St. Vitus' Dance by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People after she had been attending hospital for eighteee months. When my daughter was about fourteen years of age," Mrs. Fellows remarked to the newspaper repre- sentative, "she used to complain of pains in the stomach after eating. Doctors' medicine seemed to have no good effect, for under treatment she got, worse. She developed gastric ulcer of the stomach and was cured at the Wolverhampton Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PAINFUL TO WATCH HER.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL TO WATCH HER.</p>
        <p>, "She had only been home a little while when all the old symptoms manifested themselves, and she became a victim to St. Vitus' Dance. Again I took her to the hospital, and for eighteen months she attended once a week. But during this time she lapsed into a dreadful state. Any food caused violent pains. Twitching and nervousness manifested itself. and she could not keep the left side of her limbs still. It was painful to watoh her twitching and jumping, first her face, then her arms or legs.. Night and day she could not keep her limbs still, and the ccnstant strain made h- •-ilAy weak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CHRISTMAS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>I shall offer a ss. casket of Fry's Chocolate to the sender of the best article for a poor child. This may be any afticle of clothing. To the senders of the three next best I shall give three 2s. 6d. boxes of Fry's Chocolate. Also, as a first prize, a 2s. 6d. Fancy box and two is. 6d. boxes of Fry's Chocolate to the best in the fol- lowing Competitions : For the best Short Christmas Story, about 200 or 300 words. For the best Ji, For the best Conundrum, and for the best piece of Poetry. To all those who send any contribution, if they are not prize-winners, I shall give a littk f a ncy mirror. _ _ _</p>
        <p>All efforts must be sent me by the 14th of December.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WELCOME TO</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>John Lister, James Lister, Edith King, Alice King, Susan Hughes, Willie Scott, Annie Hat- rir}gton, Clara Whitmore.</p>
        <p>4-</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a piece of poetry, entitled,</p>
        <p>THE TWO GIRLS. Two girls I know—Jeannette and Kate— And one is always moping, The other lassie, come what may, Is ever bravely hoping. Beauty of face and girlish grace Are theirs, for joy or sorrow; Jeannette takes brightly every day, And Kate dreads each to-morrow. Joy is divine: come storm, come shine, The hopeful are the gladdest; And doubt and dread, dear girls, believe, Of all things .are the saddest. In morning's light let youth be bright, Take in the sunshine tenider, Then, at the close, shall life's decline Be full of sunset splendour. And ye who fret, try, like Jeannette, To shun all weak complaining; And not, like Kate, cry out too soon, "Oh! it is always raining!" MABEL HAYES.</p>
        <p>roar, With our captain kind and brave. In the face of death, with its fiery breath, He had stood, and so would we; For that's the way at sea, my boys, For that's the way at sea! Now let the noble words resound, And echo far and free, Wherever English hearts are found, On English shores or sea, The iron nerve of duty. joined With golden vein of love, Can dare to do, and dare to wait, With courage from above. Our captain'e shout among the flames A watchword long shall be: "That's not the way at sea, my boys! That's not the way at sea!" JOHN LISTER.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending a short tale, called, "The Advantage of a Wooden Leg." Between England and Scotland lived a man who had married a woman who had a wooden leg. His friends used to tease him about this. One day he replied. "She will be very useful at setting time; she can go on before making the holes while I drop the potatoes."</p>
        <p>HAROLD HAYES.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have never written to you before, and would like to become a member of the "Children's Corner." I always read it, and take an interest in it. lam sending you a story, and I hope you will like it. I will close, with love from</p>
        <p>WILLIE SCOTT.</p>
        <p>DIABOLO. Sin* a song of sixpence, i'pocket full of rye, Pour-and-twenty diabolists Gazing up on high. When the game was over, Who do you think did win, He who kept the devil, Limgest on the spin. CLARA WHITMORE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>IT FAILED</head>
        <p>IT FAILED</p>
        <p>A smart youngster turned up at school will his. face bound up, and all the evidence of bad attack of toothache. - -</p>
        <p>-- Perhaps the master knew his boy; for ii wasted no time in saying how sorry he was,</p>
        <p>"Where were you ye-sterday, Jones?" 11 dem3nded.- _ _ _ ,</p>
        <p>Jones mutely pointed to his bandaged fac,, but his questioner was merciless. "Did you hear me? Where were.you yester i.:ty?" he repeated. " Ferreting!" blurted Jones. " Indeed!" said the master. "So you playe. truant in order to ,go ferreting, and so con tract ecl toothache ? " Sines was silent for a moment, and thetl</p>
        <p>1-,erliaps under the impression that he couldt', very well make matters worse, he candidl;, replied: '. I contracted nuthin', sir. But I though 1: I tied my 'head -up, ye'd be askin' m questions 1 " Did you ever see a clock run? Or ink stand? Or a bottle fly? Or a man catch his .breath? Or a cracker box? Or a nidit fail? Or a bcd tick? Or a ginger snap? Or a cricket pitch? Or a man pull up a river?</p>
        <p>H. COPP.</p>
        <p>When you have occasion to utter a rebuke. let your words be soft and your argument hard</p>
        <p>When you are looking at a picture you give it the advantage of a gdod right. Be a:: courteous to your fellow-creatures as you art to a picture.</p>
        <p>That was • a good prescription given by f. physician to a &amp;tient, "Do something foi somebody."</p>
        <p>Every man can boast of one admirer.</p>
        <p>Mistress: "Let you go to evening school. Mary? Why, I thought you could read!" Mary: "Well, ma'am, I does know my letters fustrate, so long's they keep all in a row; bui just as soon's they gits mixed up into words I'm beat!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DOGS ;DISTURBING DIVINE SERVICE.</head>
        <p>DOGS ;DISTURBING DIVINE SERVICE.</p>
        <p>A Glasgow paper reports that "in a country church one Sunday 'forenoon the service was practically stopped for. a short time owing to the noise caused by a couple of dogs outside, which started worrying each other." A corre- spondent who sends us the cutting tells another Scotch story. It was mice utsual for Highland shepherds to take their dogs to church and leave them outside the pews. Two shepherds at enmity sat on opposite sides of the aisle one Sunday. Soon after the sermon began, the dogs—one a collie and the other not—seemed o enter into their masters' quarrel.</p>
        <p>One tender of the flock and then the other ?gged on his animal, and each faithful dog )heyed his master. The people at last craned heir necks over the pews, and when the dogs etually fought, not a few of the congregation yore upstanding. The minister's patience was iltimately exhausted, and su lie called to his</p>
        <p>'.hearers' and said, " Ah, weel, my britherin, I ee ye are more interested in the dog fight han in my serrnori, and so I'll cicoe the buike —and I'll bet half a crown on the collie."</p>
        <p>And the Rev. W. Melland, Rector of Port 2ynon, Swansea, ha,s rZ,corded this: Once he .v as preaching on 'the marvellous faith of the yro-Phcenician woman, to whom our .Lord ;aid, "It is not meet to take the children's )read,. and to cast it to dogs"; to which seem-- .ng rebuff she replied, "Truth, Lord; yet th logs eat of the crumbs which fall from their alasters' table." While he was uttering tliO3e words a large dog entered the church, .deliber- ttely walked up the pulpit stairs, stood for a ew moments as if expe-cting to hear something more about his .species, quietly went down again, and left the church.</p>
        <p>MAKING IT CLEAR.</p>
        <p>A young man gave a graphic deecription of a oarrow escape that .he had recently had from an enraged 'bull. , "1 eeized him by the tail," he exclaimed, "and there I was. I was afraid to hold on, and I daren't let go."' " Between the horns of a .dilemma, as it were," ventured a young laly, much interested. "No," replied the young man. "I wasn't 'Detween the horns at all, an', besides, he wasn't a dilemma; he was a'Jersey:' -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>PROUD COCK.</head>
        <p>THE PROUD COCK.</p>
        <p>One day a fox carried off one of the best _ocks. The household was roused, and all came ....tinning out. The cock said to the fox "Tell Them you can't wait." The fox opened his nouth to tell them, and dropped the cock, and ..way it flew, so Mister Fox got nothing.</p>
        <p>ELSIE TALBOT.</p>
        <p>A Chinaman thus describes a trial in our -uurts: "One man is silent, another talks all he time, and twelve wise men condemn the nan who has not said a word."</p>
        <p>But few men can handle a hot iamp chimney ,nd say there is no place like home at the same</p>
