      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PENKETH AND GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>PENKETH AND GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>PRIIfROSE LEAGUE.—A concert and dance promoted by the local habitation took place the other evening in the Great Sankey Sunday Schools, which had been tastefully decorated for the occasion with flags, bunting, &amp;c., by the ladies of the committee. There was a very large gathering, some 130 persons being present. The programme of the concert consisted of excellent vocal and instrumental music, viz. : Various selections by Mrs. W. B. Roberts' ladies orchestra; solos, "Angus Macdonald" and "A wise old owl." Miss Belle Davies.; banjo solo, Miss White ; solo, Miss Daunt; solos, "Maid of Athens" and " Because," Mr. J. Babbitt, which were given in good style. Mr. Victor Roberts. who was well received, ; supplied the humorous songs, "Slavery "and " Iu the twilight" with success, and he much amused the audience by his burlesque on "The Village Blacksmith." The Ladies' Orchestra accompanied. Short speeches were made by Councillor Fulton-Smith, Mr. Bottomley (Conservative agent for the district), Or. Bate (St. ; Helens) and Mr. Stanner (Colonel Pilkington's agent). ' At nine o'clock dancing commenced, and was kept up ; with zest until the early hours of the morning to music ; anpplied by Messrs. Ray Bros. Mr. H. Fairclough and Mr. F. NV. liertland were the M.C.'s.</p>
        <p>IST. MAny's CHURCH.—A special vestry meeting of the ; parishioners was held in Sankey school onTuesday evening to consider the offer of a gift of a stained glass window. to be inserted in the north wall of the church, in place of the present plain glass one. The window was to be dedicated "To the glory of God and in memory of ; John and Ellen Hatton Dignum, by their son Edward." The subject of the window would be three scenes in 1 the life of St. Paul. A draft copy of the proposed window was submitted and approved of. It was pro- posed by Mr. H. Pairclough, seconded by Mr. Thomas Owen, and carried, that the offer be accepted and that Et faculty be applied for forthwith. It was also pro- posed by Mr. John Ray, and seconded by Mr. Thomas I Kilshaw, and carried, "That this Vestry tender its 1 sincere thanks to Mr. Edward Dignum, for his hand- some gift—a gift which, while it perpetuated the I memory of one so highly esteemed in the parish, would . _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>also add much to the-beauty of the church.."</head>
        <p>also add much to the-beauty of the church.."</p>
        <p>CHOIR ANNIVERSARY.—on SunClit'ir -special services were held in the Wesleyan Chapel in connection with the choir. The morning service was conducted by the Rev. W. Mellor and the choir rendered the anthem, "I was glad when they said unto me." Ic the afternoon a musical service was presided over by Mr. R. U. Meldrum, of liymm. After the opening hymn and prayer Mrs. Arthur Booth sang !My heart. ever faithful" (Bach). Miss Mabel Pitt then gave a solo</p>
        <p>entitled "When I survey the wondrous cross " (Hope). A quartette was then given by Miss Johnson, Miss Atherton, Mr. Budden, and Mr. Frank Ireland, entitled "God is a spirit." Mr. G. W. Weaver gave an organ solo "Romance in D," and Mr. Ernest Walker sang "Lead, kindly light." Mrs. Booth delighted the con- gregation by her rendering "I know that my Redeemer iiveth." Mr. Ernest Walker rendered beautifully</p>
        <p>is-Wire-e-ti--hili," and Miss Mabel Pitt" Sun a my soul." in the evening the service was conducted by the Rev. E. J. Ga,wne, and the anthem entitled The wilderness" was very creditably rendered by the choir. Mrs. Booth sang beautifully "0, Divine Redeemer." Collections were taken at each service in aid of the choir funds. There were good congregations and Mr. G. W. Weaver presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURE'.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending FridAy. December 6th, was 170, viz. New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette. 97 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 73. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 87 ; Deeds of Arrangement, 84—Total 131 ; being en increase of 19. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 49 weeks of the present year is 7,405 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 49 weeks of last year was 7,921, showing a decrease ot 516.</p>
        <p>FOOD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>VISIT OF A FAMOUS BAND.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF A FAMOUS BAND.</p>
        <p>To aid Soldiers' Cottage Homes</p>
        <p>Warrington people have always taken a dcap interest in the South Lancashire Begimenr,which has very close ast,cihtions with the town, anti the visit of the band ot: the 2orl Battalion of the ianiota; regiment this week had been looked forward to with keen pleasure by many lovers of music. The hand ingratiated itself with Warringtoniens by its splendid performances on the occasion of Sir Redvers Butler's visit in February last, andzince then it has won laurels in France where the reception accorded was most flattering. It is with the laudable aim of furthering a most deserving cause that the band has been giving a series of concerts in Warrington and district during the past week. The South Lancashire Regiment has under- taken a scheme for the establishment of one or more Cottage homes, which will be dedicated to the memory of the officers, non-commissioned officers and men who lost their lives in the South African war. The inmates will be deserving soldiers selected by the Regiment in recognition of their good service with the Colours, preference being given to those who have been (a) wounded on Active Service, (b) incapacitated by disease incurred on Active Service, (c) incapacitated by disease while not on Active Service, (d) incapacitated by old age. It is to this worthy object that the pro- ceeds of the concerts will be devoted. _</p>
        <p>The concert given in the Parr Hall on Thursday after- noon was enjoyable in the highest degree, and it was to be regretted that the hall was not filled. So varied was the programme as to afford the performers the fullest scope for the display of their abilities and every item was given with precision and a musicianly taste and depth of expression which delighted the audience. At times the effect was like a great organ and throughout the members responded so intelligently to the baton of Mr. Andrew Harris, L.RA.M., the tactful conductor, that the interpretations were in every way adequate. The Grande Overture Soionelle " 1812 " in which Tschaikowsky has so vividly and impressively depicted the invasion of Russia by the French and their final defeat, was a triumph. The notes of supplication, the crash of the battle field, and the crowning victory and strains of thanksgiving with the ringing of church bells and firing of joy, were all magnificently brought out, and the applause at the close was spontaneous and pro- longed. A piccolo solo "The Lark's festival" was so cleverly executed by Sergeant E. Jenkins as to call forth a hearty encore, and the trombone quartettes "Sweet and Low " and" On the March" by Musicians W. Parker, Corporal M. Betts, A. Cleveland and Devlin proved highly popular. The Mayor showed his sympathy with the cause by attending for a short time, prior to holding the "At Home" at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>Those %ho have not already heard this splendid band should not miss the opportunity of doing so to-morrow (Sunday) evening at S o'clock at the Hippodrome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Tl 3 Shulamin."</p>
        <p>The boards at the Royal Court Theatre have this week been occupied by Mr. H. Theodore's London Company in "The Shulamite,"—the play with the strange Biblical title adapted from the novel of the same name. As a sub-title, the play passes under the ! description," A fatal lie," which certainly gives more idea of what to expect to the ordinary individual with a not very profound knowledge of the Bible. "The Shulamite " depicts life on an Aftican farm with a vividness which would do credit to an Olive Schreiner. There is only a small cast in which the principals are Simeon Krillett, a Boer farmer, Robert Waring, an English overseer in his employ, Deborah Krillett, his second wife, and Tante Anna V anderberg, his sister. On these four, the whole story hing es.Krillett is an old man with a young and pretty wife. Obviously she and the English overseer fall in love, while Tante Anna, • carefully makes mischief. These are the main facts but it is not so much the dramatic quality of the play which attracts, but the clever characterisation. In fact, after Simeon gets removed from the path of the levers " by the judgment of God "in the shape of a bullet,tbe whole thing peters out" very rapidly. The last act is a 'con- cession to the popular fancy that plays should end ; happily, and does not do justice to the story. Simeon Krillett is a fine conception of the God-fearing, Bible- : loving, and tyrahnical Boer farmer who considers his wife and his Kafars equally his sla aes. IN threat to "sjarnbok" Deborah causes her to tell him the "fatal lie" and lead him to believe that God has answered his prayers and given him a child. In a moving scene, Deborah finally confessed her deceit and Simeon is just about to shoot her when the Englishman intervenes and kills Krillett. As the Boer farmer, Mr. Algernon Hicks is splendid. Tie looks acts and speaks the part as though to the veldt born. power aect - capacity for evincing deep emotion in a natural manner are admirable, and the role is certainly a triumph for Mr. Hicks. Mr. Bartlett Garth acts con- vincingly as Robert Waring, and in the concluding scene with Deborah is particularly good. Miss Ella Thornton has clearly made a fine study of the part of Deborah and plays up to Krillett in capital fashion. Her stage demeanour in the trying scene after the confession is very praise- worthy. Compliments are due also to Miss 1 Violet Vivian whose representation of the part of Tante Anna is full of merit. The humorous element is provided by Klass, a negro, and Mempe, a Kaffir girl, both of whom are well got up and act the " darkie " well. Jan Vanderberg, illett's nephew, is pleasantly pourtrayed by Mr. G. Rammell. Those who like a play with a certain amount of originality and depth in it should not miss the concluding perform- ance of "The Shulamite " to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>There will be another splendid aitraction at this theatre next week in the shape of Mr. 0-. M. Copping's preduction of" The Black Bishop" a domestic play by Barry Williams, The play is of particular interest to Warringtonians as the late Mr. Denis Davies, of this town, used to appear as " The Black Bishop." The company will include Miss Ruth Shephard as " Ruth Oldbury " and Misv Constance Medwyn, as the twin sister " Kate Marleigh "and " Marian Swath-- coat."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>XMAS IN LIVERPOOL,</head>
        <p>XMAS IN LIVERPOOL,</p>
        <p>'That up-to-date establishment, the Sign of the Glove," kept by those well-known experts Messrs Henry Miles and Co., Church-street, Liverpool, it always prominently attractive. Just now it has scores immensely with a window display of gloves, which for extent and beauty has never been approached by any other firm. Probably there era a q uarter of million pairs in stock of all makes for ladies, gentle men, children, and even dolls. Owing to the immense business done the goods are sold at. specially popular prices. The display of reliable furs is very fine,at prices to suit all purses, the whole being in charge of a prac- tical furrier qualified to advise purchasers. In the made-up lace section there is a charming display of dainty articles suitable for gifts, including boxes of ladies' and children's handkerchiefs, motor scarves, real Maltese ties and collars, silk and wool shawls, the new kimono shoulder straps in lace, and a great variety of servants' aprons and caps at popular prices. In the outfitting showroom there is a splendid display of tea gowns, dressing gowns, dressing jackets, ladies' skirt, slips and blouses, corsets, hosiery, and very smart Norfolk golfers in all shades. In the baby linen department will be found a choice assortment of infants' dresses, pinafores, pelisses, children's coats, &amp;c. In the foreign fancy, haberdashery, and perfumery departments there is an ever changing and charming selection of inexpensive articles suitable for gifts, including ladies' belts, fans, handkerchief sachets combs, hair brushes, photo frames, trays, inkstands, and the new popular game " Diabolo " In the magnificent millinery showroom, which should on no account ba misied, there is a lovely selection of children's bad and bonnets, suitable for Christmas gifts, also ladle s' dress caps,hair ornaments,and sprays for evening wear.— Thisestablishrnent has just now an additional attraction that appeals to old and young, the learned and the simple. This is the entertainment that is given by the "Mysterious Seer who, through his hyp notised medium, performs wonderful feats of imagination oe memory; does not hesitate to answer any questions and is capable of marvellous mental gymnastics. rhe " seer " gives entertainments at intervals every day, and all purchasers are invited to witness the interesting and acsorbing seance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>An Appeal for the Ragged School.</head>
        <p>An Appeal for the Ragged School.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Again at this season of the year I make an appeal through the medium of your columns on behalf of the Ragged School and Mission, for food, cast-off clothing, toys, &amp;c. I need not remind your readers of the good work which is being done amongst the poorest of Warrington children' and I ask the good people to exhibit again that brotherliness and charity to those who live in that grimness which seems to confine them within the Insurmountable walls of adverse circumstances. They have no chance of realising the joys of Christmas unless we can show them by your help and charity that here in Warrington they are precious in somebody's sight, they have w orth in somebody's judgment and fading thatthere are hearts that remember and care for them they come to believe that these is a God who hears. There is no man or woman who can form a true conception of the joy and pleasure which a toy brings to a child's heart only those who have the distribution of some. Our friends have been very good in the past sad if they respond to the appeal in the same manner we shall be able to gladden the hearts of hundreds of Warrington poor children. On behalf the Committee. JOSEPH MARSH (Secretary.)</p>
      </div>
