      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>CHESHIRE EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Proposed Conferences</p>
        <p>A rceeting of the Cheshire Educational Committee was held on Monday at Crewe, Dr. Hodgson presiding. Pro- fessor Goncer presented the report of a special com- mittee, which recommended that conferences be held from time to time in the various educational areas for the consideration of matters of educational interest, and that the following be invited to attend :—Repre- sentatives of the County Education Committee mem- bers of the Administrative Sub-committee,and the head teachers of the schools. The Rev. J. Grant Bird said that such conferences would be a step in the right direction, and might lead to results most useful. The motion was carried. A discussion took place as to a site I.r the ow County Training College for Teachers. Lord Crewe otri!roci ten acres nenr Crewe borough and near the rtilwav station for 5.:700 an acre. It was d,,ided tosd Lord Cro.v.li to ao.ypt, 2S. 6cl. s yard for the lano.</p>
        <p>OXFORD UNDERGRADUATOS VOTE FOR DISESTABLISHNIENT.</p>
        <p>The recent Visitors' debate at the OxfOrd Union attracted a large audience. The motion, %hich was proposed by Lord Woolmer, Lord Selbornes eldest son, was to the effect that the DiseAtablishment of the Engliqii Chnrch ,ould be disist roes both to the Church mid to the nation. The spiders incladed Lord Robert Cecil, for the motion, end Mr. C. F. G. ',Wester- man, M.P., against. An exciting division resulted in e motion beim: lost by 10 votes, the fivres being 270 to 260. This is the first time the Oxford Union as over v,fed in favoor of Disestablithrnent.— "Liberator."—</p>
      </div>
