      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. To-morrow (Sunday) morning the South Lancashire band will attend the Parish Church and accompany the service. The offertory will be devoted to the Regimental Cottage Homes and the Parish Church choir fund. Particulars of the music are advertised. A humorous and dramatic recital will be given in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Thursday •evening by Mr. Edward Barnish, the eminent elocutionist. A open meeting in connection with the Wycliffe be held to-morrow (Sunday) when the speaker will be Mr. George Power of Manchester whilst the chair will be occupied by the Rev. J. Yonge. The annual display in connection with the Warring- ton Needlework Guild will be held in the Town Hall, on Thursday next. We would remind our readers that the •annual concert in connection with People's College will be held on Thursday. A Socialist lecture in connection with the Inde- pendent Labour Party will be delivered to-morrow (Sunday) in the Co-operative Hall by Councillor James Sexton, of Liverpool. At Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening the choir will give selec- tions from the' Messiah." For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt. 'Catholic Debate. The Warrington Catholic Y.M.S. debating team met Our Lady's, Liverpool on the subject of the formation of a Catholic Party. An able debate ensued, the chairman awarding the verdict to Liver- pool after a lengthy discussion with the other adjudicators. The Passion Play. A lantern lecture on the "Passion Play" at Oberammergau was given by the Rev. F. Marrs of Our Lady's, Latchford, under the auspices of the Warrington Catholic Y.M.S. in the Co-operative Hall Sankey-street on the 6th inst., before a crowded and enthusiastic audience. Suitlble instrumental and vocal music was beautifully rendered by Miss M. P. Rigby, Miss Richardson and Messrs. J. Bate, F. Warburton and S. Hazlehurst. REVOLUTION IN ELECTRIC LIGHTING.—" Electric Lamps reduce your current Bill one half,. —Agent: J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, War- rington.—Advt. Local Vicar's Preferment. The Rev. W. Pollock-Hill, Vicar of Christ Church, Padgate, has accepted the living of St. Thomas's, Ashton-in-Makerfield. The rev, gentleman is best remembered for his athletic prowess. In his 'Varsity days at Oxford he was one of the finest long-distance runners in England, holding many championships from three to ten miles. MOUNTAIN FLAX (LINUM CATHARTECUM) PILLS An agreeable aperient. Worth a trial. 9d.—Advt. Protest Meeting Against Vivisection.</p>
        <p>"Experimenting oi; dumb animals, ought it to be allowed by law ?" was the subject of an address which was delivered under the auspices of the War- rington Anti-Vivisection Society at the Co-operative Hall on Monday eveniag by Mr. Gerald Maberley, of London. The chair was occupied by Councillor Fulton-Smith, who had been asked to preside in the absence of Dr. Joseph, who is the president of the Society. Starved to Death. Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest yesterd ty at the Chapel-house, Sankey, near Warrington, on the body of Henry Gonarne, painter, of no fixed abode, which was found in a secluded spot in a field near the Cheshire Lines Railway at Sankey on Wednesday. —The evidence showed that the deceased was 53 years of age, and had been in the habit of sleeping out. Dr. Smith said that there was an entire absence of fat in the bcdy, and death was due to slight congestion of the lungs, brought about by exposure and starvation. The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt. Capital and Labour. At the meeting of the Warrington branch of the English League for the Taxation of Land Values on Wednesday evening, Mr. W. Clatworthy opened a dis- cussion on" Why is progress attended by poverty in all countries ?" He showed that the current theory that wages were paid out of capital was wrong, and that they were paid from the produce of labour. There- fore capital did not limit industry but only restricted it,its real function being to assist labour in production. The only limitation of industry was due to want of access to natural materials—the land.—The meeting was presided over by Mr. W. H. Shetburn.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. George J. Scott, of Birkenhead, has been unanimously appointed the Assistant Secretary of the Liverpool Diocesan C.E.T.S. from about fifty appli- cants, and will commence work on January Ist. Mr. Scott succeeds the Rev. P. W. Pheysey, who has been appointed the Secretary in the place of the Rev. J. Hirst. Mr. H. Sherburn, of Stocktrm Heath, has been successful in gaining the full technological certificate of the City and Guilds of London Institute in iron and steel manufacture. The qualification necessary for obtaining this certificate is success in advanced metallurgy and inor.-ranic chemistry (theoretical and practical), and iron and steel in manufacture—First Class in Honours Grade. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9.§d. &amp; 1/li. Of Ghemists.—Advt Novel by Miss Crosfield. Warrington people will he interested to learn that Miss Truda Crosfield, the Borough Member's talented sister, has entered the ranks of novelists with a romance entitled "A Love in Ancient Days," pub- lished by Mr. Elkin Mathews, Vigo-street, London, W., at Os. We have not yet been able to peruse the story, but it treats of the period in the sixth century when Saxon invaders were conquering the Southern part of England and driving inland the native Britons, and concerns the fortunes of two British maidens, Avanwy and Patra, who become captives. Miss Crosfield has clearly made a careful study of history relating to these early days in the evolution of a great nation, and the story lacks nothing in local colour or graceful diction. The hook contains 400 pages and is well hound in cloth and gilt. Eight illustrations are contributed by Mr. W. B. Virollen, R.I. Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; never fails. 1/14, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Lee's Tapestry Works. i CASE SETTLED. 1 In the Lancashire Court of Chancery' at Manchester- A dance promoted by the employees of these works before Vice-Chancellor Leigh Clare, on Tuesday, ttO was held in the Conservative Club Assembly Room hearing of the case of Turner v. Turner was resumed. 1 last evening. Admirable arrangements had been . When tha proceedings commenced there was a :lug. made and the affair proved a complete success. • gestion from the Vice-Chancellor that some steps , ' should be made towards a settlement privately. The Miss Marie Houghton. counsel agreed, and it was eventually announced by Mr, This talented young Warrington singer has been Radford that matters had been settled. They would selected to play the title-role in the romantic opera not state the terms in open court. " Cigarette 'at the Bolton Grand Theatre, next week, The Vice-Chancellor said he was glad the parties bad , under the auspices of the Bolton Amateur Operatic settled it. If they had left it to him to decide it might : have been disastrous whichever way he decided it. "I 1 Society. eUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THE CLOCK FOP. BliT am very glad you have had the sense to settle it," con- eluded the Vice-Chancellor. BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. , 4s.</p>
        <p>Arts and Crafts Exhibition. The members of the recently-formed Warrington ! -- Arts and Crafts Society have reason to be encouraged by the success of their first exhibition which has been held in Garven House, Sankey-street, during the past MR. J. A. SEDDON M.P., AND THE LORDS. week. The Society's aim—a very laudable one which Speaking at Burtonwood, on Wednesday night, Mr. .1. is meeting with a fair amount of support—is A. Seddon, M.P., said the Labour party had other duties besides legislation. There was at the present time a art as applied to craft, and to show how great deal of maladministration, by which much injury principles of beauty may be brought to bear upon was done. So far as the House of Lords were concerned the home ,surroundings and industrial productions. the Labour party had no fears. When the House of The exhibits number over 150, paintings being tne Lords saw organised labour had made up its mind on a principal feature. Other exhibits include lace, certain policy the House of Lords would be brought to embroidery, applique copper repousse stained and its knees, as was the case with the wicked Trades carved wood, wrought iron, stencilled hangings, way of Labour, it would be so much the Disputes for the Disputes Bill, if ever the House of Lords stood in the designs for interior decoration, and book-bindings. i House of Lords. A vote of confidence n Mr. Seddon , The exhibition was opened on Monday afternoon by was carried unanimously. Alderman Bennett and will continue open this after- CHEMICAL WORKS EXPLOSION. noon and evening. Miss H. Fairclough has acted as . i At half-past two on Saturday afternoon a violent hon. secretary. explosion occurred at Messrs. Sullivan's chemical Wedding at St. Paul's Church. works, Moss Bank, Widnes. It transpires that in the new nitre sulphate plant, which adjoins Messrs. There was a pretty wedding at St. Paul's Church, on Thursday, when Miss Margaret Gardner, first PilkirNton's works, is a huge pot for hea