enshaw, W. Winstanley, A. Haining, Ball at the Parr Hall. T. Antwis, A. Greenway, J. Balshaw, E. Britch, G. E. The Parr Hall was the scene of a brilliant function Warburton, J. Stokes, G. F. Ashton (clerk), T. Cooke, W. R. DePrez, and J. Pearson (relieving officers).—T e last evening when a public ball in aid of the South Clerk reported that the County Call for the curre t Lancashhe Regimental Cottage Homes was held. A half year was £8,009 19s. 10d. The last precept w scene of great beauty was presented, the hall having £8,313 13s. 10d. , showing a decrease of £253 13s. 10d. been most artistically decorated under the supervision There was also a decrease of £l2l 9s. 10d. for of Mr. Ralph Johnson. Union Jacks, muslin and general police, £llB 17s. Bd. for district police, , other light materials, and choice plants had been £l3 16s. 4d. for special education purposes. ' arranged to the best advantage and the whole made ' —Mr. J. Spruce said the doctor and Mr. Dansey had up .a striking picture. For the additional comfort and met a fairly attended meeting of the Visiting Com- mittee and the committee resolved "That it be a enjoyment of the dancers a parquet floor had been recommendation the board that a fully certificated specially laid for the occasion and the innova- nurse be advertised for n the place of Nurse Crowton tion gave general satisfaction. The retiring who had resigned."—Mr. Morris seconded and the rooms were tastefully decorated and the intro- resolution was adopted.—Mr. Morris proposed "That duction of electric lights with vane-coloured the salary of the Boys' Industrial Trainer be increased shades in the corridors heightened the effect. This from £25 to £3O per annum." He said that he had work had been carried out by Mr. H. Bibby. The received information from the House Committee that attendance numbered well over 100 and an excellent Mr. Boyce (the trainer) had given them every satis- faction and the salary would be the same as his pre- programme of 20 dances was gone through to the decessor had.—Mr. .1. H. Davies seconded and the strains of the famous string band of the 2nd Battalion resolution was carried. South Lancashire Regiment. The members of the Committee, who also acted as M.C's, were :—Mr. W.: WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL. P. Rylands, Colonel Edelsten, Major Bundock, Captain' THE CO3T OF GAS AND WATER. Crosfield, Mr. H. Morgan, and Captain Osborne, the ' His Worship the Mayor (Alderman S. Owens) pre- last-named gentleman being the energetic hon. sided over the monthly meeting of the Widnes Town oCounciltion on J'en cht.sedefi. yt il—intru. tmoving the secretary. During the evening supper was served,Lewis,Ls ewsaid in ;,llet Mr. Powys catering in his best style. The bar had adoption normal health. No minutes, of enteric feevwenr was been arranged under the stage, Councillor Rogers in the hospital.—Alderman Gossage asked the corn- being in charge. mittee to seriously deal with the question of slum , property in the town, and report on the same.—Mr. : Lewis said the committee was always alive to their , responsibility in the matter, which was under con- sideration. 'Their efforts would not he relaxed. Alder- man Timmis asked for the Mayor's indulgenoe to , allude to the price of gas and water, which has caused so much discussion in Widnee, in connection with their new Parliamentary Powers Bill. He wished to put before the Council the figures by which the minimum ckarges are arrived at. The ques- tion arose out of a special meeting which the Council held the other day, when it was stated that Aae eclat. 11111. to make, hut WILR 1-0499' 901 d for power in the town at 11d. In the same way, water was produced at a cost of 3d. and sold for 2d. This was absolutely true. Those prices were permitted as a matter of policy. The net cost per thousand of gas was 11ff of a penny. They had an up-to-date gasworks, and a great deal of money had been sunk in plant. The lighting time in the town represented about six hours which meant that for only six hours of twenty-four were they earning money. The greater the quantity of gas passing through mains the greater would be the reduction in cost all roand, therefore they could afford to supply it at a lower cost and yet at a profit to themselves. In consequence of this low charge seventy-seven gas engines were using supplies during the day. With respect to the waterworks, the consumption of water was 21,000,000 per week. The reservoir at Pox Hill cost £40,000, and holds 12,000,000 gallons of water. The committee decided that, instead of spend- ing more money in reservoirs, it was far better to leave the water in the natural reservoirs,and spend the money on a more efficient and powerful pumping plant. They had spent £lO,OOO on Stocks well, and £15,000 on duplicating their engines, and by this means they could offer it cheaper. It was only the surplus over so many millions of gallons used at works that was sold for 2d. so it worked out well. This was really good business, and at the council meeting at which it was discussed it was passed unanimously.—The Mayor, in referring to the Finance Committee's minute, said that Widnes took the sixtieth place in the list of ninety English towns with respect to its rates.</p>
