      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>WOUNDING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Sessions on Tuesday, Frank Riley (25), labourer, pleaded not guilty to (1) unlawfully wounding William Downes, seventeen yegrs of age, at Newton-le- Willows, on November 24th, and (2) unlawfully wound- ing James Downes, father of the youth, at the same time. Mr. Griffiths, who prosecuted, said the older Downes was asleep on his hearth on the Sunday after- noon when a man (not in custody) rushed into the house and struck him. Downes rose to defend himself, and prisoner thereupon entered and joined forces against Downes, stabbing him twice in the head and on the thumb with a knife, the youth, who took his father's part, receiving less serious injuries from the knife wielded by prisoner. When the elder Downes got possession of the poker, he cleared his two assailants out, but the men not in custody wrecked the window from outside, a female witness remarking that he had once thus treated a whole row of houses, his excuse being that he was mad. Prisoner's defence was that he entered the house of the prosecutors, to restore peace' was brutally kicked, and not responsible for the injuries sustained by the prosecutors, which injuries he ascribed to the man not in custody or to a fall on broken crokery. He denied that he had a knife. Recalled by the jury, who had deliberated long, Dr. Walker (Earlestown) said he did not think it possible that the wound on the face of the youth Downes could have been caused with anything but a blade. Found guilty on a reduced charge, prisoner was sentenced for four months.</p>
        <p>M. SEDDON, M.P., ON TARIFF REFORNI AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>Mr.J.A. Seddon,M.P.,had a very busy day on Saturday as he was present at a Socialist tea party, and a trade union meeting at Earlestown, and a political electing at Hollinfare. Speaking at Hollinfaro he dealt at length with the question of Tariff Reform. All sorts ef snares, he said, had been set by Tariff Reformers for Mr. Balfour to make him into an apostle of Mr. Chamberlain's doctrine. But they had a long way yet to go before they snared him. He (51r. Seddon) chal- lenged anyone to show him where, at Birmingham recently, Mr. Balfour suggested Tariff Reform as a main plank of the Tory platform. His speech was compar- able only to an amateur debating society. He would broaden ths basis of taxation. Quite so, but there were only two ways of doing it—by direct or indirect taxation. Indirect taxation would fall upon the general community, being a tax on commodities which were the staple articles of diet. This would hit the very poorest, and be a gross injustice. (Hear, hear, and applause.) How, then could Mr. Balfour broaden the basis of taxation and not inflict hardship on those least able to bear it? He would not dare to propose a further tax on the incomes of the rich. The Labour Party contended that Free Trade had proved its advan- tages to the people of this nountry, and in Protectionist El Dorados in different parts of the world unemploy- ment and had trade were not unknown, as the case of America to-day showed. Tariff Reform was the Juggernaut of the Trusts. and America was " trusted " from the cradle to the grave. (Applause.) Speaking at Earlestown, Mr. Seddon said if they were to judge by signs of the times they were in for a period of depres- sion in trade. The great boom in trade, of which the workers had received little benefit, was coming to an end. With trade unions there was generally a decline in membership at such times, but this was a vital mistake. At such a period their organization should be the strongest to enable them to withstand the tyranny of employers, who sought to decrease wages during a trade decline. He urged them to stick fast to their trade unions. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S WEDDING. Letter to the Mayor.</p>
        <p>The Mayor has received the following letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.</p>
        <p>Riviera Palace Hotel, Penzance, Dececuber 11th, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>His Worship the Mayor of</head>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor of</p>
        <p>Warrington, - Town Hall, Warrington. My Dear Mr. Mayor, We were so greatly pressed for time on the day of our wedding in Paris that I was unable to do more than send you a brief telegram. So that now that we have arrived at what is our destination for the first part of our wedding trip I want to tell you how deeply grateful we both felt for the very comprehensive and generous, and truly kind message you sent us. Such a message would have been highly appreciated and valued at any time, but above all on such an eventful occasion. May I beg you therefore Mr. Mayor to convey to the members of the Council, and indeed if you will be so kind, to all friends outside as well as inside the Council—so widely represented in your telegram—and to accept yourself my most sincere.and respectful thanks. It will shortly be my pleasant duty to answer many kind messages for which one feels more than grateful. But lam sure, Mr. Mayor, you will understand that I feel in a very special degree honoured by the message you sent me. It was more than kind of you, and I thank you not only on my own account, but still more because of the natural gratificatioa it afforded my wife. With kind regards to your Worship and grateful acknowl&amp;dgments to everyone, in which my wife joins me.</p>
        <p>Believe me always, Very sincerely yours, A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>PRIORY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>PRIORY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Annual Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>The spacious assembly room of the Conservative Club, Sankey-street, was filled to its utmost capacity on Tuesday evening, the occasion being the annual prize distribution and concert in connection with the Priory School. Much praise is due to the Misses Williams for the excellent way they had trained the girls for the occasion aud for the efficient manner in which the arrangements for the concert were carried out. The prizes were presented by Alderman Tinnion. Miss WILLIAMS said that the four girls who sat for the Oxford local examination bad all passed, and she believed that was the fourth year in succession that their candidates had been the only girls successful here in the senior local examine- tion. There were sixty-nine pupils on the school 1 register this term, and she was sorry to say a good many ; of them were away that night through various unfor- I tunate circumstances.</p>
        <p>After complimenting Miss Williams on the success of the scholars, Alderman Tinnion distributed the prizes as follows:</p>
        <p>Dorothy Hill, class prize, drawing prize, and music prize, Royal Drawing Society certificate, Division V. • Elsie Carter, fifth class prize' French prize, Royal Drawing Society certificates, Honours Division 1111., Pass, Division V. Oxford local junior certificate; May Tinnion, fourth class prize, Royal Drawing Society certificate. Division 11.; Dorothy Crampton, third class prize; Alice Dennett, second class prize; Annie Carter, upper first class prize; Fergus Ferguson, lower first class prize ; Marjorie Matthews, Kinder- garter prize; Isobel Ferguson, conduct prize; Nancy Wade, attendance prize; Muriel Matthews, examination prize, Royal Drawing Society certiflcAtes, Division I. and If; Constance Hall, the Associated Board's certifi- cate for rudiments of music ; Nellie Taylor, Trinity College senior certificate for pianoforte playing; Bessie Whitiovv,Trinity College senior certificate fr pianoforte playing ; Ida Johnson, Oxford local senior certificate ; Marian Ellison, oxford Local Senior certificate ; Doris Williams, Royal Drawing Society's certificate, Honours, Division I. and 11., Pass 111. ; Mina Johnson„Royal Drawing Society's certificate, Honours, Divisions 1. Pass IL; Ada Collin, Royal Drawing Society's certifi- ' cate, pass Division 1., Honours, Division 11. ; Nellie Wade, Royal Drawing Society's certificate, Divisions I. and 11. Oxford Local Preliminary certificate ; Hilda 1 Gamon, Trinity College Junior certificate pianoforte ' playing, Royal Drawing Society's certificate, Division 1.; Lucy Isherwood, Royal Drawing Society's certifi- cate, Division 1., Honours II.; Doris Taylor, Trinity College Preparatory certificate for pianoforte playing. The concert programme included musical items by , the Misses Hall, Hilda Gamon, i. Chidlaw, D. Owen. I B. Whitlow, D. Williams, D. Pitt, and Plinston, and ' "The Merry Milkmaids" presented by the pupils in a very bright and tuneful manner.</p>
        <p>--- "Feb° de se," which, we've been told, Is meant for suicide, Applies to Jones, who caught a cold, Neglected it, and died; Carelessness won't make old bones, Nor health to us secure. When you've a cold, don't follow Jones— Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>COMPANY PROMOTING AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Rumen Action;</p>
        <p>Mr. James Dodds a Witness.</p>
        <p>I In the Lancashire Court of Chancery at St. George's ! Hall, before Vice-Chancellor Leigh-Clare on Monday, the case of Turner v. Turner came up for trial. It was • an action by the firm styled Turner and Company, glass manufacturers, St. Helens, against Joseph Robson Turner and Robert Newman Turner for specific per- formance of an agreement made between plaintiff com- pany and the defendants for the sale of the goodwill and property of defendants ; damages were claimed in lien of specific performance. Defendants admitted negotiations to sell their business to a company to be ; formed, but denied that they had entered into the ! contracts alleged by the plaintiffs, or that such agree- ments were ratified or adopted by the plaintiff company or were capable of being legally ratified, and counter- claimed for possession of their old works in Claughton- ! street, St. Helens, from which they claimed they had been wrongfully ejected and excluded by the plaintiff company, in March, 1906. Mr. Radford (instructed by Messrs. Batty, Ford, and Buckley, of Manchester) were for plaintiffs, and Mr. Grant and :11r. Courthope Wilson (instructed by Messrs. Oppenheim and Sons, St. Helens) were for the defen- dants.</p>
        <p>Mr. Radford said defendants carried on business as glass merchants in St. Helens, and had a warehouse in Claughton-street. Desirous of embarking on the manu- facture of sheet-glass and to purchase a portion of the property of Tubes, Limited, they applied to Mr. James Dodds, an old friend, with the view of raising capital. Mr. Dodds approached Mr. William Walker and gr. Francis J. Crossfield, and subsequently it was arranged to promote a company with a capital of £30,000 divided into 2,000 ordinary shares of £lO each and 1,000 vendors' shares of £lO each. Plaintiff's case was that the busi- ness of Messrs. Turner was to be taken for £2,500, plus the ascertained value of their works, and that in or about November, 1906, it MB distinctly agreed between the two Turners and the directors of Turners, Limited, that the Turners were to receive .C 6,000 in shares for their business and were to find another £l,OOO. In reply to the Vice-Chancellor, Mr. Radford stated that it was an informal meeting of the directors at which this agreement was made. There was no minute of the meeting, In corroboration of the agreement Mr. Radford said tbat the company paid £5OO deposit for the purchase of the Tubes works, extended the works, and had paid liabilities of Turner and Company to the extent of £3,991, which was in excess of what they received by £278 us. The company had also paid interest on the mortgage debt of £1,475. Everything had been done by the company which they undertook to do except the satisfaction of this £6,000. Mr. Grant : They thought they were going to make a very fine thing out of it. It was not until afterwards they found they were not. Mr. Radford read correspondence, which he claimed proved that the contract was duly made: Mr. James Dodds, one of the plaintiffs, Penketh House, Warrington, spoke to being applied to by defen- dants for assistance to purchase the Tubes premises at St.HelensJunction,and to the new company. Turuer and Co., Limited,being formed with a capital of £30,000. At one of the interviews Joseph Turner mentioned in a quiet way that the value of their business would come out at about £7,000 and they thought they would like to have £lO,OOO for the business in shares. Joseph Turner was always willing that the business should be taken over at what it was worth in the books.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Grant.—Witness was secretary of Pearson, Knowles, and Co., and his secretarial duties were consieerable. He was a director of about ten companies in all. He admitted that part of the duty of a secretary was to keep minutes of directors' meetings. With the exception of the meeting of the 10th Novem- ber, he took minutes of all the directors' meetings of Turner and Co.,Limited. Counsel, handing to witness the minute book, asked him did he mean to say that the minutes of each meeting were written at different times. Witness insisted that they were. Mr. Grant: The meeting in November was a most important one to the company—a vital one in fact. Mr. Dodds: It has turned out to be. Why did you not take minutes of that meeting ?—.l was very ilk at the time. - - - -</p>
        <p>Why didn't you get 3omeone else to take the minutes? I might have done that. Referriug to one or two letters which had not been produced, counsel asked witness if it was his custom to keep some business letters and destroy others. Mr. Dodds: Not intentionally. Mr. GrAnt asked if witness could mention tl," aato on which the agreement ot traustef was arranged. Mr. Dodds replied that it was clearly understood that Messrs. Turner should transfer their business to the company when formed at a value to be fixed. "We were agreed all the time on this." Counsel preased for dates of the agreement, and witness asked that his word be accepted. His reputa- tion was good enough. He ought not to be insulted. His Honour: Mr. Grant does not wish to insult you. I will take care of you. Mr. Grant: You are something of a lawyer, Mr. Dodds?</p>
        <p>Hi 3 Honour: Now you are insulting Mr. Dodds. (Laughter.) Mr. Grant: You prepared the memorandum of the articles of association, Mr. Dodds? I always thought that to be legal work? Mr. Dodds: Same lawynrs do:a know the slightest thing about it. (Laughter.) Witness denied that he wanted only himself and friends to find the capital. "We came in to help two young men struggling forward, and we have lost a lot of money." Before Mr. Dodds' cross-examination had concluded, the court adjourned, and the further hearing took place at the Manchester Court. ST. HELENS LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>A mass meeting of members of the St. Helens Labour Party and the local branches of the Miners' Federation was held at St. Helens Town Hall on Tuesday night. when the principal speakers were Mr. T. Glover, M.P. for St. Helens; Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P. for lace ; Mr. Harry Twist, and local Labour leaders.—Councillor Rudd proposed a resolution expressing confidence in the Labour party, and urging the continuAnce of efforts to secure an Unemployed Act and an old age pension scheme.—Mr. T. Glover supported. Dealing with the question of compensation he asserted that he was certain there were employers in that town who were not carrying out the Act of Parliament, and who were not paying their workers the money they ought under the Act. Boys who got hurt were under the Act entitled to a minimum of 10s. a week, but he knew that certain firms in the town were trying to force , their employees to accept a now scheme under the Com- pensation Act, by which a boy whose wages were ss. a week got compensation at the rate of is. 6d., and men who earned .£2 a week got 15s. That scheme was not as good as the Act, and the workers and tho registrar ought not to sign it. Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., commented on the harm worked by the Compensation Act of 1897. The serious and wilful misconduct clause had robbed tens of thousands of workpeople, widows, and children. A Labour amendment to the Act of 1906 was the means of curtailing the operations of that plea, and in future when a man was killed in the course of his employment it would not be possible for an employer to say that ti e dead man bad been guilty of serious and wilful miscon- duct.—The resolution was most heartily carried.</p>
        <p>EMPLOYNIENT OF CHILDREN IN SFIOPS. BATCH OF SUMMONSES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Twelve hairdressers and managers of boot shops, etc., were summoned at St. Helens on Monday,. under the Employment of Children Act, 1903, for employing boys under fourteen years of age after the hours of 9 0 p.m. These were the first cases taken locally under the Act, and the precautions had given very great annoyance to the tradesmen, who complained to the Bench that the police had not warned them. The accused were : Horatio Smith, hairdresser, 54, Bridge-street (three summonses) ; Henry Smith. hairdresser, 2, Liverpool- road- John Barr, hairdresser, 79, Tontine-street (two summonses) ; William Kay, 80, Liverpool-road (two summonses) ; Robert Verdill, manager, 80, Liverpool- road; James Kean, grocer, 8. Liverpool-road ; Oliver C. Cooper, 97, Kirkland-street; Edward A. Goodwin, ironmonger, Church-went ; Henry Webb, glass dealer, Church-street ; Jame% B. Warren, boat dealer, 2, Market-street ; Ernest E. Jodes, manager, 26, Chur3h- street ; Thomas Molyneux, fruiterer, 149, Wegtfield- street—All the defendants except Mr. J.V3. Warren were ordered to pay Ss. 6d. in each case.</p>
        <p>"NATURE'S EDIBLE BLACK PEARLS."</p>
        <p>It sounds funny, but it's a doctor's description of Currants, which are a delightful sweetmeat, a nourish- ing food, and a natural medicine, all in' one. Ask your Groc9r for a fne copy of the booklet, " Currauts—A few Tasty Recipes." It i full of delightful recipes for the Christums season. The follow- ing is a specimen :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>CURRANT PATTIES.</head>
        <p>CURRANT PATTIES.</p>
        <p>Ingredients: One ounce of cornflour, four ounces of currants, one lemon, four ounces of castor sugar,quarter of an ounce of butter, short crust paste. Method: Line some patty pans with thinly-rolled- out short crust paste, providing paste covers for each. Mix the cornflour with a gill of cold water, stir in tne grated rind and juice of lemon, the currants, and the sugar; cook all together for five minutes, stirring all the time, and add the butter; mix well, fill the paste- lined patty pans with the preparation, wet the edges of the paste, put on the covers, brush over witlA sweet milk, and bake twenty minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication Inuit in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not later than Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>Anti-Vivisection.</head>
        <p>Anti-Vivisection.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l regret that owing to other business I was unable to attend the meeting held on Monday evening to assist the " Anti-Vivisectionists." To the human mind can anything be more terrible than the needless torture of dumb animals? I think not. One may be excused of being sentimental upon this matter. If so, thank God I am. As advanced by a few speakers, where does the good to humanity come in? " Cui bono ?" (to whose good). 'Tis mayhap all very well for tyros undergoing their apprenticeship to witness the sufferings of the poor dumb animals who, be it remembered, the Almighty has created in common with ourselves. Further, what possible advantage can accrue? In my opinion the whole thing is not Amy unnecessary but most cruel.We have a very worthy Association deserving of all support called" Prevention of Cruelty to Animals." Then I ask why this torture is allowed to go on? Surely by legislation it may be stopped.—l am, Sir, yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>SIGMA.</p>
        <p>Mr. Grayson's Socialism. Is Interest Robbery ? Sir,—l have just returned from the Parr Hall Socialistic meeting and while the impressions are fresh I would like to jot them down. As soon as Mr. Grayson rose the meeting became very much alive, and he kept it alive for a full hour and a half by a speech of great power, interest and earnest- ness. I thought he wasted a good deal of time at the beginning in dealing with a leaflet distributed I presume at the door, which attacked Socialism mainly on the ground of the atheism of Robert Blatchford. The pamphlet was a foolish one, and scarcely merited the time spent upon it. What interested me most was the part of the speech which touched on the economic aspect of the question as it affected interest and capital ; this unfortunately was very brief. He countered the fear that many have that they would have their savings confiscated by say- ing that Socialists have no desire to confiscate any honestly earned savings. Suppose a man had honestly saved £lOO Mr. Grayson would leave this untouched, the owner could wrap it up in a stocking, spend it, or do anything he liked with it except lay it out to interest, because " Interest is robbery." If the owner took it to a lank and arranged with the banker to give him 21. per cent. interest then" he would have the Socialists to reckon with." " Money never made money," said the speaker. "The banker would lock the £lOO in his safe. " Would the clerks go with the watering can to wet the safe, with the idea of making the sovereigns grow and multiply ?" (Applause and laughter.) At this point many of us were all attentive because we expected that the speaker would go to the root of the matter and show that "interest was robbery." Our disappoint- ment was great when either intentionally or inadver- tently the speaker never finished the argument but turned back to the offending pamphlet. Now the point is all important,it is one on which the whole thing rests. If interest is robbery then Socialism will gain the day. If individual capital can be proved to be the enemy of labour and not the friend then the sooner we put it in State chains and under State control the better.</p>
        <p>To go back to the £lOO. Mr. Grayson knows very well that the banker only locks the money in his safe temporarily else how could he afford to pay interest? , In an hour after it is paid in it may be paid out and be exchanged by the borrower for some instrument of pro- duction which a worker or producer desires to procure ' to help him in his work. Suppose the original possessor of the £lOO, instead of putting it into the bank to be exchanged for some article of production, exchanges it himself for a horse and cart. He may either use the horse and cart to help him in his own labour or he may let it out on hire. If he lets it out to hire, the hire he receives includes wear and tear and interest on his £lOO. Is this interest robbery? Does he rob the man who hires his cart or does he assist his labour? It is clear if the hirer does not consider it to his advantage to have the use of this horse and cart he would not hire it, but be satisfied say with his own barrow. It is bemuse. the one who hires knows that after paying the interest or hire he will have a greater result for his labour than if he had paid no hire and been content to work only with his barrow. But what of the man who owns the horse and cart, the " bloated capitalist " as he is generally called in Socialist nomenclature, why should he have hire or interest? Simply because if he had his horse and cart at home he could use it in helping him to do his own work. He sacrifices this advantage and gets instead the hire or inter Pot. vehe4.. *t,,A.4 is the robbery? Capital if it is stored labour pure and simple (and nothing else is capital) if hired to help labour oppeora to be the friend of labour and not the robber.</p>
        <p>Until Socialists can convince us by calm reason that this is not so they cannot expect us to adopt their remedy, which in our opinion puts capital in a position in which it will lie less able to help labour,and labour in a position in which it will be less effective. This does not prevent us sympathizing with them in their earnest desire to improve social conditions. We simply wish to be sure that what is proposed is a wise remedy, and not a means of introducing greater evils. Will Mr. Grayson or some Warrington Socialist help to make this plain? C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Peokoth Lodge, December 10tb, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>MR. VICTOR GRAYSON, MP. AT WARRINGTON. Reply to Critics.</head>
        <p>MR. VICTOR GRAYSON, MP. AT WARRINGTON. Reply to Critics.</p>
        <p>Mr. VICTOR GRAvsoN,M.P., recently returned repre- sentative of Colne Valley, was the principal speaker at a Socialist demonstration held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening under the auspices of the Independent Labour P,-rty. There was a fair attendance, Mr, William Pook (secretary of Gasworkers and General Labourers' Union) presiding. There were also present on the platform :—Mr. Victor Grayson, M.P., Coun- cillor McLachlan, of Levenshulme, Councillor Ball, Mrs. Wilkinson (Board of Guardians), Mrs. Pendry (secretary of the Women's Branch of the 1.L.P.), Mr. J. Haley (Distress Committee), Messrs. J. Podrnore, R. Pierce Williams, J. Heywood, C. Lee, G. Highley, G. Reeves, W. Bagot (secretary of the Irlam 1.L.P.), J. Marsden, A. Didsbury, A. Wallace, J. Wallace and W. Speakman (Earlestown 1.L.P.) The CHAIRMAN said the anti-Socialist campaign could be better termed the anti-truth campaign. Almost every charge agsinst Socialism, with regard to unemployment, breaking up of home, immorality and so on, could be burled at the present system of individualism. Under Socialism any machinery that lightened labour would be welcomed. It would have a tendency to shorten the hours of labour instead of throwing men out of work. As to Socialism breaking up the home, individualism had never supplied the people with houses. (Applause.) The average number of persons in London whose homes were common lodging-houses, was over 30,000. Every night 11,000 were to be found in casual wards. Not far from War- rington pigs were living under conditions which would be a God-send to thousands of human beings. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>SOCIALIS \I AND ATHEIS</head>
        <p>SOCIALIS \I AND ATHEIS</p>
        <p>Councillor NlcLictiLxx said the fear manifested in the hysterical tone of their opponents and the tuothods by which they tried to discredit Socialism were sufficient evidence of the corning break-up in their opponents' ranks. When thieves fell out honest men came by their own. (Applaum) The people were ' entitled to demand the abolition of the present vile system in the name of their common humanity, and to assert their right to a re-constructive policy which would bring hack to the:u the rights of which they had been robbed. Socialism had a clean record and a gospel of hope to humanity which the worn out philosophies and creeds of their opponents could never show. As members of society they had the right to live and to work anththey were not going to rest until those two inalienable rights of humanity were the heritage of every individual. (Applause.) They refused any Conger to be sweated for low wages, to be starved and insulted by oppressive conditions and inadequate feeding, to be stifled to death in vile slums. The speaker dealt at some length with the question of Socialism and Atheism and replied to a pamphlet which had been extensively circulated before the meeting. He said Christ was a Socialist and preached the right to live, the brotherhood of.men, and the dignity of labour. Who were the Atheists ?—the Socialists who were helping the poor and wanted to make every man com- fortable and self-respecting, or tho men who lived by usury—which Christ condeamel—upon the labour of other men, taking from them two-thirds of the wealth they produced, keeping them in poverty, and living on their parasitic extractions from the earnings of men? (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MR. GRAYSON'S SPEECEI. " TORYISM STONE DEAD."</p>
        <p>Mr. Vrc roR GRAXSON, who had a flattering recep- tion, delivered a long speech sparkling with humorous touches. He said that since the anti-Sonialist campaign had ben started, their tongues had been wagging. (Laughter.) Those who cared most for the health of their party warned them not to go so fast, but with such delightful game in the field as the Duke of Rutland, Lord Londonderry and Lady Londonderry, not to speak of gramophones and handbills, they could not resist dissecting the Conservative corpse. (Laughter and applause.) He had no hesitation in saying that</p>
        <p>- TORYISM WAS STONE DEAD. Some Torie might say it was a very lively corpse, brit the Socialist party had been behind the scenes and</p>
        <p>soi,b AGE PENSIONS. Mr. W. CHRIMES asked Mr. Grayson whether he had any practical scheme for providing old age pensions? Mr. GRAYSON said he shared the expressions used by Socialists at various times as to how the money could be obtained to provide pensions. Mr. Asquith could not find the means because they were too near to him to he seen. He (Mr. Grayson) would take Mr. Asquith to the House of Lords, the members of which could be made to provide more than was necessary without having to part with anything they had ever earned. (Applause.) He would tax the unearned increment of the rich, the fat money bags of the men who were taking the product of the people's labour on the land, for the purpose of providing the pensions. He warned the workers that Mr. Asquith would probably try to make them pay for the pensions out of their own pockets. He concluded by asking all to come within the ranks of the Socialist Party. They would find most congenial company, for they had all kinds of supporters from countesses down to poets, pimps and burglars. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>watched the battery at work. (Laughter.) They gave the corpse convulsive twitches, and said dramatically "See it's alive ! " (Laughter.) But as a political philosophy Toryism had been stone dead for ten years. They had no need to fight Toryism nor even individualism in these days. He had taken part in many debates, and he had sharpened his weapons only toe find his opponents had risen and said : Igo nine-tnths of the way with you; I believe in the nationalization ! At a meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday another discussion, arising out of the of railways, I.believe in the municipalization of all natural monopoly." After a humorous reference to the recent slander action conflicting views of Tories on Socialism, Mr. Grayson officer, occupied a consi against the resident medical derable time. replied to a pamphlet of an anti-Socialist nature which; It was reported that at a meeting of the House Corn- had been distributed outside the hall, and said he I mittee the resignation of Probationer Nurse Dinnis was was glad some persons were willing to aid the accepted. - Socialist campaign by spending their money on the 1 The Board confirmed this decision and agreed that publication of such literature. Such opoonents did not Nurse Dinnis should be allowed to leave at once with- attack the broad set of economic principles put up for I out serving a notice. their attack but they abused the plaintiff. They would The CLERK read a letter from Dr. Flitcroft in which !he said : " I hereby give you notiee of my resignation rather cast about for personalities of the most despic- able character and tell the People that it was not any !of the office of resident medical officer, to take effect dream the Socialists wanted to realize,but that they were according to legal requirements." gross, materialistic, unpoetical persons who Ns anted to ! Mr. BllaiNeE, as chairman of the House Committee, take people's money, to tear their wives and children moved that 