      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-12-14_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>WORKHOUSE SCANDAL.</head>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE SCANDAL.</p>
        <p>DR. FLITGROFT RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>THE N.S L. DEBATE.</p>
        <p>A Reply to Captain Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The correspondent who contributed some notes on the Peace Debate writes :</p>
        <p>The generous prominence given to my notes a fort- night ago tempts me to say a further word upon the subject of the debate. Captain Crosfield in his reply to my criticism is ready to admit that his comparison of the soldier to the fireman or ambulance man is imperfect. My argument is that it is altogether mis- leading. Before any analogy can be traced between the soldier and fireman some analogy must be traced between an outbreak of war and an outbreak of fire. The street accident and the fire are la the nature of; accidents and the function of the fire or ambulance man is, if I may use the word, redemptive. But war is not accident. It is the organised conflict- , of nations and the poor soldier is the tool by which the conflict is made. In Captain Crosfield's analogy of a conflagation the only thing to which the soldier can be compared is the fuel that is consumed ; to compare , him to the man who extinguishes the fire is mischievous argument. Another misleading sentence is that in which he suggests that by giving all men a military training we shall lessen the risks of war from the reason that men who know the terrors of war • will be the last to desire it. If soldiers were the war makers and were consulted before an outbreak, I can very well believe that their influence would be given on the side of peace. But can Captain Crosfield . mention any crisis at which the wishes of the soldiers have been consulted? Was any plebiscite taken in the army. or from the electorat., before the South African War</p>
        <p>We are obliged to look facts in the face and recog- nise that the armies and navies are tools in the hands of Statesmen to use as they will. Eight years ago we were plunged into costly war in South Africa by a Statesman whose subsequent career has inspired us with anything but confidence. The memory of South Africa and the admissions which have since been made concerning it must give us pause before we embark upon any scheme for the making of an unlimited number of soldiers.</p>
      </div>
