        <p>WARRINGTON. , i , Wycliffe Young People's Society. The weekly meeting was held in the Memorial Hall, Mr. W. Brown presiding. The evening was devoted ito impromptu speaking n which nearly all the mem- bers took part, and a very enjoyable time was spent. i Arpley Fields Flooded. ; On Sunday the river Mersey overflowed its banks- and almost the whole of Arpley fields were flooded, The road underneath the railway bridge was impassable , for several hours, and the bottom portion of Chester road was also under water for a considerable time. PUBLIC BENEFFI`e—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Agent for HARRIS'S BACON, ASHLEY. Sankey-st.—Adv t Death of Mr. B. James. . The death occurred on Sunday after a long and painful illness, borne with great resignation, of Mr. Benjamin James, for the past 30 years foreman in the Goods Department of the Cheshire Lines. He was much respected by a large circle of friends and his decease is much regretted. Temporary Authority. At the Borough Police Court, oil Monday, Mr George Eastwood was granted permission to sell at: the Crown and Sceptre, Buttermarket-street, in,lieu of Mr. Thomas Walsh, and Mr. James Lawton was ' granted temporary authority to sell at a beerhouse in ' Fennel-street in place of George Eastwood. ; ; Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. 1 ‘COAGULINE,"KLINIK,"TENASITINE.'--Cements for broken and other articles.—Advt. I Personal. 1 ; Mr. George J. Scott, of Birkenhead, has been : unanimously appointed the Assistant Secretary of the ; Liverpool Diocesan C.E.T.S. from about fifty aPpli- i cants, and will commence work on January Ist. Mr. . Scott succeeds the Rev. P. W. Phevsey, who has been : appointed the Secretary in the place of the Rev. J. i Hirst. ; Law Society Examination. 1 At the November examination for honours of candi- dates for admission on the Roll of Solicitors of the ; Supreme Court, the examination committee. recom- ; mended the following, among others, for distinctioi:— ; Second class. W. B. Forshaw, (Mr W. J. FOrshow, of , Warrington). Musical Successes. i Victoria College of Music,Manchester Centre, by Dr. i Bentley. The following pupils of Miss Maud Jennings, . A.V.C.M , of 140, Wilderspool Causeway, passed , I very successfully: Miss Mabel Houghton, pianoforte i playing 2nd division, 88 marks, Ist class with honours; Miss Minnie Warburton, 88 marks, Ist class with ! honours; 2nd division, Bruce Starkey, 90 marks, ' , Ist class with honours. MORE LIGHT AT LESS COST.—" Osram " Electric i Lamps, do this and more. Full details from the , local agent.—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. ) Penny Popular Concerts. An exceptional treat was provided for the patrons of these popular concerts on Saturday evening when I an entertainment arranged by Mrs. Hubi Newcombe ' was given. Miss Mary Brundrett pleased her hearers ! with her fine renderings of "The lonely road "and ; "Love's coronation," and Mr. W. Ireland was loudly I applauded for his songs "The Star of Bethlehem" j and "The tin gee-gee." The Misses Dorothy Truslovej and Florence Brown, and Messrs. Joseph Forrester ! and Wallace Ray also won high enconiums with their; songs. Messrs. Ireland and Ray were deservedly , encored for their duet "Flow, gently, Deva." Miss , Lillie, Locke, who acted as accompanist, treated the 1 audience to several cleverly. executed pianoforte solos. —The Rev. T. Miller presided, and during an interval spoke upon the evils of gambling. 1 'LINSEED COMPOUND 'of 40 years' proven efficacy 1 for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.— Advt 1 P.S.A. Meetings. I Mr. R. Atkinson presided over a well-attended , meeting of the Wycliffe Society on Sunday. Mr. T. A. i Williams; of Bristol, gave an address upon " Vivisection." showing the great suffering to which dumb animals were continually being put for scientific purpoees. He contended that the torture of the animals had not saved a single human life. ' No ' good purposes could be served by cruelly butchering dumb animals. The sermon on the Mount was referred to when Christ said, "Blessed are the merciful." Let them teach the people cleanliness, purity within and without, and good sanitary laws ; which were of far greater importance, than ill-treating, dumb creatures. Mr. Bradley, and Mr. 3.owen also took , part in tho meeting Mr. H. Plumnton sang well "The people that walked in darkness" and "Behold I stand at the door.' Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ.—The weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey- street. It being an open Sunday there was a large gathering. The chair was occupied by the vice- president, Mr. Freeman. The Rev. T. Miller gave an address on "The Bible, its present position as a hand- book to modern thought and scientific progress." Prayer was offered by Mr. Brown, the lesson was read by the President. and the orchestral band rendered great service to the meeting which proved a source of instruction and spiritual benefit to all present. 'LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp; Colds.-. Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Market Thefts. - At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, four boys named George Keddie, 25, Alcard-street ; John Hickman, 39, Ashton-street • Isiah Mason, 172, Wellfield-street ; and Harold Morrie, 70, Back Dallam lane, were charged with stealing from the lock-up stall of the Peacock Bazaar Company in the Market- place, about 1.15 on Friday afternoon, two Christmas figures. three Christmas stockings, and one glass ball, value 6d. —All pleaded guilty. —Defendants had nothing to say, and they were each ordered to receive six strokes with the birch rod.—Keddie and Hickman and William Chilton, 7, Rodney-street, and William Leigh, 30, Haydock-street, were now charged with breaking into the lock-up stall of the Peacock Bazaar Company some Lime between November 30th and December 4th, and stealing a quantity of articles value 13a. 4rl.—Chilton and Leigh pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Place, chief inspector, said he hoped the Bench would assist him in trying to put a stop to this stealing, which had I been going on for some time. As soon as the boysl from the King-street, Heathside, and Wycliffe Schools came out at nights, they swarmed into the market in i scores. It was no use trying to catch them, and they I called people all sorts of names. They went to the ' receptacles for rubbish and threw it at each other. and made the market not fit to be seen. The police 1 and himself had done all they could, but they could not manage them.—The defendants were each ordered to receive six strokes with the birch rod, sad to be kepi at the Police Station until six o'clock. " OSRA.M " ELECTRIC LAMPS.—Double the light AIM SUSANNAH FELLOWS. at half the cost. See Windows.—Agent: J. S. Cured of St. Vitus' Dance GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. . by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt "The St. Vitus' Dance became more acute, and Presentation to Mr. and Mrs John Upson. affected her all down the left side,and it made our hearts The Silver-street Sunday School Men's Bible Class ache to look at her. We did not know what to do as held their social gathering on Saturday evening, in we had tried several doctors, the hospital, and Many the Bewsey-road Lecture Hall. About 120 members medicines without deriving benefit. One day we read and friends assembled to spend a delightful evening, of a case similar to Susie's. It was that of a youth at Among the artistes who contributed to their enjoy- Wolverhampton who had been cured by Dr. Williams' ment were Miss Emily Taylor, Mrs. Sefton, Mr. Sefton Pink Pills. My husband and I decided to try them and Mr. H. Plumpton, each of whom sang several with our dear daughter. songs, for which the accompaniments were supplied ASTONISHMENT AND JOY. by Mrs. Forman. The 