at sea!" of eight children. The father is a shoemaker and a good worker, but thrown out of employ- So each one did as he was bid, ment through the introduction of some special And into boats we passed; . machinery in the factory where he worked. He While closer came the scorching .flame, has tried day after day to get but with- And our captain was the last. work, out avail. The mother has been very ill, and Yet once again he dared his life, ordered by the doctor to keep absolUtely •quiet. One little lad to save; The children can't go to school on account of Then we pulled to shore from the blaze and lack of clothes, and frequently have only bread to eat all the week. Now, this is perfectly true, as I know the family very well, and, in fact, took the poor woman into my own house for a week, so that she should have rest and goo,d food. These are the kind of people I am trying to help, so I do hope you will try and help to provide some clothing for them. Tours affectionately, UNCLE NED.</p>
